LAB.pages = [
 {"id": "L2-obs-climate", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "weather", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide009_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Steep granite peaks with snow and glaciers above a lake, southern Andes", "cap": "A · southern Andes"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Bare, eroded, pale badland ridges with no vegetation, Atacama Desert", "cap": "B · Atacama Desert"}], "img": "img/L2/slide009_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · two climates", "q": "Which landscape has more chemical weathering going on, and what's doing most of the work in the other one?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> glaciers, snow, rain, a lake and some vegetation, all on very steep, actively uplifting mountains.</li><li><b>B:</b> no plants, no water, sharp wind-carved ridges, pale bare rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A has far more chemical weathering, because it has water. It also has strong physical weathering from glaciers, gravity and steep relief. B is almost all physical: wind blowing sand around. Parts of the Atacama have seen almost no rain for millions of years, which is why minerals that should dissolve in rain, like nitrates, sit there perfectly stable. The opposite extreme is Antarctica: everything is frozen, there's no free water, so there's almost no chemical weathering at all.</p>", "e": "So A has far more chemical weathering, because it has water. It also has strong physical weathering from glaciers, gravity and steep relief. B is almost all physical: wind blowing sand around. Parts of the Atacama have seen almost no rain for millions of years, which is why minerals that should dissolve in rain, like nitrates, sit there perfectly stable. The opposite extreme is Antarctica: everything is frozen, there's no free water, so there's almost no chemical weathering at all."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-granite", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist beside a quarry face of pale, heavily jointed granite with orange-red iron staining along the joints"}], "img": "img/L2/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Manuka open cut, south of Cobar", "q": "This used to be a hard granite. List three things in the photo that tell you it's deeply weathered.", "seen": "<ul><li>Abundant joints and fractures cutting the whole face, now the paths water followed.</li><li>A pale, bleached colour overall: iron has been leached out of the rock.</li><li>Red-orange hematite staining concentrated along the fractures, where that iron reprecipitated.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Up close (slide 17) only small cores of fresh granite survive; the rest is quartz plus clay, soft enough to dig. Weathering attacks from every fracture inward, so the last fresh rock is always in the middle of the blocks.</p>", "e": "Up close (slide 17) only small cores of fresh granite survive; the rest is quartz plus clay, soft enough to dig. Weathering attacks from every fracture inward, so the last fresh rock is always in the middle of the blocks."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-profile", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Open pit wall showing a reddish-brown upper layer, a pale bleached band, and darker iron-stained rock below, with water ponded in the pit floor"}], "img": "img/L2/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Boundary South pit, Manuka mine", "q": "Log this pit wall from top to bottom. What are the layers, and what did water do to make them?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Top:</b> iron-rich, red-brown soil and weathered rock.</li><li><b>Middle:</b> a pale, bleached zone where you can still see bedding, but the iron is gone.</li><li><b>Bottom:</b> brown, iron-altered limestone, then fresh rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">The water table moved up and down through this profile many times. Each time it carried dissolved iron with it, stripping the bleached zone and redepositing iron above and below. Iron is the element groundwater moves most visibly. Notice also that the iron isn't evenly spread: a fault through the pit controls where the high-iron bands sit.</p>", "e": "The water table moved up and down through this profile many times. Each time it carried dissolved iron with it, stripping the bleached zone and redepositing iron above and below. Iron is the element groundwater moves most visibly. Notice also that the iron isn't evenly spread: a fault through the pit controls where the high-iron bands sit."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-core18", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "profiles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core that is crumbly, pale orange and white, with no visible rock texture"}], "img": "img/L2/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · diamond core at 18 m, Copper Deeps, Drake Goldfield", "q": "This is 18 m down a drill hole. What rock was it?", "seen": "<p>Honestly, you can't tell. There's no structure left at all, just kaolinite and hematite. That's the point: in Western Australia you can drill through more than 100 m of intensely weathered material before you hit anything that looks like rock, because the profile has been soaking in groundwater for millions of years. Immobile-element chemistry (later in this lecture) is how you work out what the parent rock was.</p>", "e": "Honestly, you can't tell. There's no structure left at all, just kaolinite and hematite. That's the point: in Western Australia you can drill through more than 100 m of intensely weathered material before you hit anything that looks like rock, because the profile has been soaking in groundwater for millions of years. Immobile-element chemistry (later in this lecture) is how you work out what the parent rock was."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-gossanAu", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "gossans", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geologist standing in a shallow pit dug into red-brown gossan among scrubby trees"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of hard, dark red-brown gossan blocks next to a geological hammer"}], "img": "img/L2/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · an unknown gossan, southern Cobar field", "q": "No prospector or miner ever recorded this outcrop. It turned out to carry visible gold all through it, yet there's no sulphide left at all. Why did the gold survive when everything else rusted?", "seen": "<p>Gold doesn't oxidise. The pyrite or pyrrhotite that once hosted it reacted completely to goethite, but the gold grains just sat there, and if anything got relatively richer as the rock around them lost mass. Two lessons: undiscovered gossans are still out there to find, even in well-explored districts like Cobar; and a gossan sample is assayed for metals, not judged by its looks.</p>", "e": "Gold doesn't oxidise. The pyrite or pyrrhotite that once hosted it reacted completely to goethite, but the gold grains just sat there, and if anything got relatively richer as the rock around them lost mass. Two lessons: undiscovered gossans are still out there to find, even in well-explored districts like Cobar; and a gossan sample is assayed for metals, not judged by its looks."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-tsumeb", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "deep", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Deep blue azurite crystals with pale green malachite", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide036_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Emerald-green crystals on white matrix", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide036_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink rhombohedral crystals in a cluster", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L2/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Tsumeb specimens", "q": "All three are secondary minerals from the oxide zone. Which metal does each carry, and why is C pink?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> azurite (deep blue) with malachite (green): copper hydroxyl carbonates.</li><li><b>B:</b> dioptase: copper silicate, famously vivid green. Tsumeb's are among the best in the world.</li><li><b>C:</b> smithsonite, zinc carbonate. Zinc has no colour of its own; the pink comes from a trace of cobalt.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Bright colours in an oxide zone mostly mean copper (blue, green). Colourless or white usually means lead or zinc carbonates and sulphates (cerussite, smithsonite, anglesite) unless a trace element tints them.</p>", "e": "Bright colours in an oxide zone mostly mean copper (blue, green). Colourless or white usually means lead or zinc carbonates and sulphates (cerussite, smithsonite, anglesite) unless a trace element tints them."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-kelly", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "deep", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Open pit wall with labelled bands: acid-leached zone, goethite halo, and enriched band"}], "img": "img/L2/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Mount Kelly, north of Mount Isa", "q": "Mount Kelly was mined for its oxide zone only. Which labelled band was the ore, and how do you think it was processed?", "seen": "<p>The darker <b>enriched band</b> on the right, rich in hematite and copper oxides and carbonates (malachite, azurite). The acid-leached zone has been stripped of metal, and the goethite halo marks iron moved by groundwater around it. The ore was crushed, stacked on a pad and sprayed with acid for a few months, then the copper-bearing solution was pumped off: heap leaching, about as simple as metallurgy gets. The deposit sits where the Mount Kelly Fault meets the regional McNamara Fault, and the mineralised band runs for about 500 m along strike.</p>", "e": "The darker enriched band on the right, rich in hematite and copper oxides and carbonates (malachite, azurite). The acid-leached zone has been stripped of metal, and the goethite halo marks iron moved by groundwater around it. The ore was crushed, stacked on a pad and sprayed with acid for a few months, then the copper-bearing solution was pumped off: heap leaching, about as simple as metallurgy gets. The deposit sits where the Mount Kelly Fault meets the regional McNamara Fault, and the mineralised band runs for about 500 m along strike."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-altered", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "alter", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale, soft-looking drill core with a chalky texture", "cap": "A · altered volcanic tuff, core"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide042_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section under the microscope with bright high-coloured grains", "cap": "B · thin section, Woodlark Island, PNG"}], "img": "img/L2/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · two altered rocks", "q": "In A, what happened to the feldspars? In B, the bright, high-interference-colour mineral is a classic alteration mineral. Which one, and is it a hotter or cooler alteration than A?", "seen": "<p>A: the feldspars have all gone to clay, because acidic hydrothermal fluid attacked them. The core is pale, chalky and soft. B: the bright mineral is epidote, which forms at higher temperatures than clay (argillic) alteration. Two rocks, two very different fluid temperatures, and you can read that from the minerals alone.</p>", "e": "A: the feldspars have all gone to clay, because acidic hydrothermal fluid attacked them. The core is pale, chalky and soft. B: the bright mineral is epidote, which forms at higher temperatures than clay (argillic) alteration. Two rocks, two very different fluid temperatures, and you can read that from the minerals alone."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-propphyl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "assemblages", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core of green rock with scattered pale and dark fragments", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide047_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a pale grey-white rock with orange iron spots", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L2/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · green rock and pale rock", "q": "Name the alteration in each, using only colour and what you'd expect to find in it.", "seen": "<p>A is <b>propylitic</b>: the green comes from chlorite and epidote, and you'd expect scattered pyrite. B is <b>phyllic</b>: pale because feldspar and ferromagnesian minerals have gone to fine white mica and quartz, with orange spots where pyrite has weathered to iron oxide. If green rock, think propylitic, and look for pyrite.</p>", "e": "A is propylitic: the green comes from chlorite and epidote, and you'd expect scattered pyrite. B is phyllic: pale because feldspar and ferromagnesian minerals have gone to fine white mica and quartz, with orange spots where pyrite has weathered to iron oxide. If green rock, think propylitic, and look for pyrite."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-jaguar", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "zoning", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of dark green basalt pillows with paler bleached margins and sulphide along the pillow rims"}], "img": "img/L2/slide063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Jaguar VMS deposit, Western Australia", "q": "These are Archaean pillow basalts, over 3 billion years old. Where did the sulphide go in first, and why there?", "seen": "<p>Sulphide first replaced the margins between the pillows and filled joint cracks, because those are planes of weakness that fluid can get into. Strong chlorite forms around pillow boundaries where flow was weaker. Closer to the vent, flow was intense enough to bleach the basalt and replace whole pillows with chalcopyrite and pyrite. It's a replacement deposit: you can watch it eat the rock from the outside in.</p>", "e": "Sulphide first replaced the margins between the pillows and filled joint cracks, because those are planes of weakness that fluid can get into. Strong chlorite forms around pillow boundaries where flow was weaker. Closer to the vent, flow was intense enough to bleach the basalt and replace whole pillows with chalcopyrite and pyrite. It's a replacement deposit: you can watch it eat the rock from the outside in."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-cowley", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "measure", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological cross-section with drill holes through zones of epidote plus albite, quartz plus pyrite, quartz plus sericite and quartz plus sericite plus chlorite"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide065_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark green drill core with pale green epidote patches", "cap": "1 · distal"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide066_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core streaked with white quartz and dark pyrite stringers", "cap": "2 · ore zone"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide067_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey, fine-grained siliceous drill core", "cap": "3 · above the ore"}], "img": "img/L2/slide065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · Cowley Hills VMS, near Woodlawn", "q": "Describe each core in one line, then predict what happens to AI, sodium and barium as you go from 1 to 2 to 3.", "seen": "<ul><li><b>1:</b> dark basalt with patchy epidote, chlorite and albite. The primary basalt texture survives in thin section; pyroxene has gone to epidote and chlorite, Ca-plagioclase to albite. AI about 50, CCPI about 90. Epidote plus chlorite tells you you're too hot and too far away.</li><li><b>2:</b> intense stringer quartz and pyrite, more than chlorite, with chalcopyrite. It's no longer recognisably basalt. AI jumps to about 85, CCPI about 95, and Na<sub>2</sub>O drops to 0.14% because sodium is mobile in hot fluid and gets stripped.</li><li><b>3:</b> intense quartz plus sericite, just above the ore: mostly quartz with lots of muscovite. Barium (754 ppm) and copper (687 ppm) are high even though it isn't ore, because in VMS systems barium precipitates after the sulphides, higher in the sequence.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">Down the drill hole, AI and CCPI dip and then jump straight up at the mineralisation and fall away after it. Sodium does the opposite. And if a supposedly distal unit plots in the \"favourable\" footwall field, go back and look: you may have misinterpreted it, and there may be mineralisation nearby nobody has found.</p>", "e": "Down the drill hole, AI and CCPI dip and then jump straight up at the mineralisation and fall away after it. Sodium does the opposite. And if a supposedly distal unit plots in the \"favourable\" footwall field, go back and look: you may have misinterpreted it, and there may be mineralisation nearby nobody has found."},
 {"id": "L2-obs-veins", "kind": "obs", "lec": 2, "sec": "vector", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L2/slide069_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark rock cut by a thin pale vein with only a narrow pale margin", "cap": "A · Sn-W vein"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide070_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark purple-brown rock with pale bleached halos along fractures", "cap": "B · bleaching"}, {"src": "img/L2/slide070_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark sulphide-rich core with angular pale bleached host rock fragments", "cap": "C · silver ore"}], "img": "img/L2/slide069_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 2 field task · narrow alteration around veins", "q": "Why can't you use a big alteration halo to find veins like A, and what does the pale rock in B and C tell you?", "seen": "<p>Vein A carries significant tin and tungsten, but its alteration is only a few centimetres wide. Hydrothermal vein deposits usually have very narrow, discrete alteration zones, a metre at most, so alteration isn't a long-range vector for them. What you do get is <b>bleaching</b>: acidic fluid strips iron and magnesium, and the silicon and aluminium left behind make the rock pale. Nothing was added, so the rock looks white because the colouring elements are gone. When you start seeing bleaching like B, you're close. In C, the dark ore carries about 2000 ppm silver, and the host fragments inside it are completely bleached.</p>", "e": "Vein A carries significant tin and tungsten, but its alteration is only a few centimetres wide. Hydrothermal vein deposits usually have very narrow, discrete alteration zones, a metre at most, so alteration isn't a long-range vector for them. What you do get is bleaching: acidic fluid strips iron and magnesium, and the silicon and aluminium left behind make the rock pale. Nothing was added, so the rock looks white because the colouring elements are gone. When you start seeing bleaching like B, you're close. In C, the dark ore carries about 2000 ppm silver, and the host fragments inside it are completely bleached."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-chert", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "sed", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide006_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of pale angular chert fragments packed together", "cap": "A · outcrop"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Sliced slab with pale, grey and red chert clasts of different textures in a red matrix", "cap": "B · sawn slab"}], "img": "img/L3/slide006_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · chert breccia, outcrop and slab", "q": "The slab has clasts of several colours and textures, and a red matrix. Is the red colour part of the breccia-forming process?", "seen": "<p>The different clast colours are different generations or textures of chert, all replacing carbonate. The red is almost certainly hematite from later weathering and oxidation, not part of the original brecciation. Always separate the event that made the texture from the events that stained it afterwards (Lecture 2).</p>", "e": "The different clast colours are different generations or textures of chert, all replacing carbonate. The red is almost certainly hematite from later weathering and oxidation, not part of the original brecciation. Always separate the event that made the texture from the events that stained it afterwards (Lecture 2)."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-quarry", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "sed", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Quarry face with labelled collapse breccia, in situ breccia pipe, collapse breccia pipe and stratiform breccia zones"}], "img": "img/L3/slide009_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · limestone quarry wall", "q": "Find the collapse features in this real quarry face. Which labelled body shows inward-dipping, collapsed beds, and where would you expect the flat base?", "seen": "<p>The collapse breccia pipes on the right are the V-shaped, chaotic bodies where roof rock fell in. The in situ breccia pipe has broken but barely moved. At the base, the stratiform breccia marks the level of the dissolved layer: a flat, sharp floor.</p>", "e": "The collapse breccia pipes on the right are the V-shaped, chaotic bodies where roof rock fell in. The in situ breccia pipe has broken but barely moved. At the base, the stratiform breccia marks the level of the dissolved layer: a flat, sharp floor."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-faultbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "fault", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide011_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Banded grey shale and sandstone cut by a vertical zone of broken rock", "cap": "A · bedded sequence"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale green angular blocks separated by thin dark seams", "cap": "B · close-up"}], "img": "img/L3/slide011_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · two fault breccias", "q": "In A, how do you know the broken zone is a fault and not a sedimentary breccia? Classify B.", "seen": "<p>A: the broken zone cuts straight across the shale-sandstone bedding as a narrow, steep band, and the beds either side are intact. That geometry says fault movement. B: the pale blocks are large, angular and still nearly fit together, with only thin seams of matrix between them: a crackle breccia, very little rotation.</p>", "e": "A: the broken zone cuts straight across the shale-sandstone bedding as a narrow, steep band, and the beds either side are intact. That geometry says fault movement. B: the pale blocks are large, angular and still nearly fit together, with only thin seams of matrix between them: a crackle breccia, very little rotation."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-magbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "magvol", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with pale subrounded andesite clasts", "cap": "A · Payandayan volcano, West Java"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide016_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Brown sulphide matrix with dark rounded clasts", "cap": "B · Sudbury, Canada"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide016_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Black chromitite fragments in bright green and white cement", "cap": "C · Coolac, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · three magmatic breccias", "q": "Two of these have a matrix that was itself molten. Which two, and what's the green cement in C?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> subrounded andesite clasts, some with reaction rims: they were broken while the magma was still partly liquid and reacted with it.</li><li><b>B:</b> well-rounded clasts of the host norite in a matrix of sulphide. The sulphide was a melt too, a separate sulphide liquid, so the matrix was molten.</li><li><b>C:</b> chromitite broken while still in a magmatic state, then cemented by later fluids that grew green uvarovite (chromium garnet) and grossular.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">So A and B had molten matrices. C's cement came later, from fluids. Rounded clasts and reaction rims are your clue to a hot, magmatic setting.</p>", "e": "So A and B had molten matrices. C's cement came later, from fluids. Rounded clasts and reaction rims are your clue to a hot, magmatic setting."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-volbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "magvol", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Large dark boulder made of packed angular lava blocks", "cap": "A · block breccia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Drill core of angular mixed rock fragments of many types", "cap": "B · Kulnura Quarry, NSW"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide021_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Boulder of pale and grey fragments in a fine ashy matrix", "cap": "C · pyroclastic breccia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop with large rounded and angular boulders in a fine matrix, very poorly sorted", "cap": "D · debris flow"}], "img": "img/L3/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · volcanic breccias", "q": "Which one was driven by gravity rather than heat, and what's odd about the clasts in B?", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> is the gravity one: huge range of clast sizes, very poorly sorted, a debris flow (epiclastic). <b>B</b> is a phreatic breccia: lava hit groundwater, the steam blast shattered the country rock, and the mix was injected upwards as a breccia dyke. That's why most of B's clasts are country rock, not volcanic. A is a block breccia from a lava flow breaking itself up, and C is pyroclastic: many rock types in an ash matrix.</p>", "e": "D is the gravity one: huge range of clast sizes, very poorly sorted, a debris flow (epiclastic). B is a phreatic breccia: lava hit groundwater, the steam blast shattered the country rock, and the mix was injected upwards as a breccia dyke. That's why most of B's clasts are country rock, not volcanic. A is a block breccia from a lava flow breaking itself up, and C is pyroclastic: many rock types in an ash matrix."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-hydbx", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "hydro", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink granite fragments in white silica with purple fluorite", "cap": "A · Mount Surprise, QLD"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide025_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey granite clasts separated by brown sphalerite", "cap": "B · Aspasia mine, Georgetown"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rounded dark clasts in white quartz with grey stibnite", "cap": "C · Hillgrove, NE NSW"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale altered granite clasts in a crushed granite matrix stained brown", "cap": "D · Mount Turner porphyry"}], "img": "img/L3/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · hydrothermal breccias from Queensland and NSW", "q": "In which of these is the cement itself ore? And in D, what is the matrix made of?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> granite blasted apart, cemented by fine silica (chalcedony) and purple fluorite carried by the escaping volatiles.</li><li><b>B:</b> granite clasts cemented by sphalerite: the cement is the ore. The clasts are visibly altered.</li><li><b>C:</b> quartz crystals nucleated around the clasts, then stibnite (antimony sulphide, the ore) filled the gaps.</li><li><b>D:</b> a milled-matrix breccia: highly altered granite clasts in granite that was itself crushed to powder by the fluid, with former sulphides now oxidised to goethite.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">B and C have ore as cement. Some epithermal deposits are hosted entirely in breccia rather than veins; Kelian in Kalimantan is the classic example.</p>", "e": "B and C have ore as cement. Some epithermal deposits are hosted entirely in breccia rather than veins; Kelian in Kalimantan is the classic example."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "banded", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide034_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Brassy chalcopyrite with thin bronze pyrrhotite layers", "cap": "A · Jaguar, WA (VMS)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide034_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale pyrite with darker sphalerite bands", "cap": "B · Scuddles, WA (VMS)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey slab with dark and light sulphide bands that are gently folded", "cap": "C · Mount Isa, QLD (SEDEX)"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark slab with fine wavy pale bands", "cap": "D · Lady Loretta, QLD (SEDEX)"}], "img": "img/L3/slide034_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · four banded ores", "q": "The bands in C are folded. Does that mean the ore was deformed by later tectonics?", "seen": "<p>Not necessarily. At Mount Isa the galena-sphalerite bands and the shale between them are folded together in small, soft-sediment folds caused by compaction while the mud was still wet. The sulphide and the shale were behaving as one wet sediment, which is more evidence that they formed together. Tectonic folds would also deform later veins and would have a consistent axial plane across the outcrop.</p>", "e": "Not necessarily. At Mount Isa the galena-sphalerite bands and the shale between them are folded together in small, soft-sediment folds caused by compaction while the mud was still wet. The sulphide and the shale were behaving as one wet sediment, which is more evidence that they formed together. Tectonic folds would also deform later veins and would have a consistent axial plane across the outcrop."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-lamext", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide040_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White quartz with many fine dark grey laminations", "cap": "A · Mount Turner porphyry, QLD"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide040_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale banded quartz and carbonate vein with fine dark bands", "cap": "B · Masuparia, Kalimantan"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide041_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Vein with quartz crystals grown straight across from wall to wall, with brassy sulphide", "cap": "C · Bingham, USA"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide041_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Sandstone with thin parallel rusty quartz veins", "cap": "D · Budgery mine, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide040_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · laminated and extensional veins", "q": "Which two are crack-seal veins, and which way are the crystals pointing in C?", "seen": "<p><b>A and B</b> are laminated crack-seal veins: open, seal, open, seal, many times over. In A the dark laminae are molybdenite. B is a classic ginguro vein from an epithermal system: colourless quartz, off-white ankerite and sooty black sulphide bands. That sample ran around 1500 g/t silver and 150 g/t gold. Find ginguro banding while exploring an epithermal system and you get very excited. <b>C and D</b> are extensional: planar, with crystals grown perpendicular to the vein walls because the rock simply pulled apart. In C, chalcopyrite, bornite and gold filled in later.</p>", "e": "A and B are laminated crack-seal veins: open, seal, open, seal, many times over. In A the dark laminae are molybdenite. B is a classic ginguro vein from an epithermal system: colourless quartz, off-white ankerite and sooty black sulphide bands. That sample ran around 1500 g/t silver and 150 g/t gold. Find ginguro banding while exploring an epithermal system and you get very excited. C and D are extensional: planar, with crystals grown perpendicular to the vein walls because the rock simply pulled apart. In C, chalcopyrite, bornite and gold filled in later."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-space", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with a vein whose centre is filled with dark grey molybdenite", "cap": "A · Bingham, molybdenite infill"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide043_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark euhedral sphalerite crystals with pale quartz filling around them", "cap": "B · Strauss open cut, Drake"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide045_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink altered monzonite cut by many crossing grey and black veinlets", "cap": "C · Goonumbla, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · space-fill and stockwork", "q": "A and B are both space-fill veins, but their order is opposite. Explain, then say why companies love C.", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> follows the usual order: quartz lined the walls first and molybdenite filled the open centre later. <b>B</b> is reversed: euhedral sphalerite crystals grew first into open space, and quartz filled around them afterwards. You can tell because the sphalerite has crystal faces, which only grow into open space. <b>C</b> is a stockwork: many crossing veins of early quartz and later quartz-chalcocite in brecciated, altered monzonite. Lots of veins means lots of separate mineralising pulses, which is exactly what makes porphyries and epithermal systems big. They form by intense hydrofracturing as overpressured fluid exsolves close to the surface.</p>", "e": "A follows the usual order: quartz lined the walls first and molybdenite filled the open centre later. B is reversed: euhedral sphalerite crystals grew first into open space, and quartz filled around them afterwards. You can tell because the sphalerite has crystal faces, which only grow into open space. C is a stockwork: many crossing veins of early quartz and later quartz-chalcocite in brecciated, altered monzonite. Lots of veins means lots of separate mineralising pulses, which is exactly what makes porphyries and epithermal systems big. They form by intense hydrofracturing as overpressured fluid exsolves close to the surface."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-magvein", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "veins", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Olive-green dunite with a single black sinuous chromitite vein", "cap": "A · Kondyor Massif, Russia"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide046_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey phyllite with a large pale quartz vein on the left and a cross-cutting shear vein", "cap": "B · Tallebung Sn-W, NSW"}], "img": "img/L3/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · magmatic and complex veins", "q": "A isn't hydrothermal at all. How did a chromitite vein form in dunite? And what's the sequence in B?", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a magmatic vein: dense chromite crystals segregated and aggregated into a monomineralic vein within the silicate host. Magmatic veins are rare; chromitite in dunite and pyrrhotite-pentlandite in ultramafic rock are the usual cases. <b>B</b> records two events: extension opened a large quartz-cassiterite vein, then later shearing cut a shear vein across it.</p>", "e": "A is a magmatic vein: dense chromite crystals segregated and aggregated into a monomineralic vein within the silicate host. Magmatic veins are rare; chromitite in dunite and pyrrhotite-pentlandite in ultramafic rock are the usual cases. B records two events: extension opened a large quartz-cassiterite vein, then later shearing cut a shear vein across it."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-meta", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "meta", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide048_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Coarse, shiny grey galena and sphalerite crystals", "cap": "A · Hera, level 615"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide048_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Galena with long, ribbon-like stretched crystals", "cap": "B · Woodcutters, NT"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide049_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Banded rock of dark pyrrhotite-rich bands and pale quartz bands", "cap": "C · Mount Windarra, WA"}], "img": "img/L3/slide048_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · metamorphosed ores", "q": "What does each tell you about what metamorphism did?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>A:</b> very coarse, recrystallised galena-sphalerite. Coarse grain size is the giveaway for remobilisation.</li><li><b>B:</b> highly strained, ribbon-like galena stretched during deformation: galena flows ductilely.</li><li><b>C:</b> this was a banded iron formation of hematite and chert. Metamorphism, with sulphur introduced, converted the hematite to pyrrhotite and recrystallised the chert to coarse quartz. The banding survived; the minerals didn't.</li></ul>", "e": "A: very coarse, recrystallised galena-sphalerite. Coarse grain size is the giveaway for remobilisation. B: highly strained, ribbon-like galena stretched during deformation: galena flows ductilely. C: this was a banded iron formation of hematite and chert. Metamorphism, with sulphur introduced, converted the hematite to pyrrhotite and recrystallised the chert to coarse quartz. The banding survived; the minerals didn't."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-durch", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "meta", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide051_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with pale angular siltstone clasts in grey fine-grained sulphide", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L3/slide055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with clasts of quartz and orange garnet in fine grey galena", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L3/slide051_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · durchbewegung at Hera", "q": "B contains orange garnet in quartz clasts, inside fine galena. Why is garnet a surprise in a sulphide vein, and what does it tell you?", "seen": "<p>Garnet needs high temperature. These quartz-garnet clasts formed deeper in the deposit, in hotter skarn conditions, then were broken off and carried up a shear vein inside ductile, fine-grained galena. So one sample records an early hot event, a later deformation, and the galena flowing around the debris. A shows the same texture with bleached siltstone clasts in sphalerite-galena.</p>", "e": "Garnet needs high temperature. These quartz-garnet clasts formed deeper in the deposit, in hotter skarn conditions, then were broken off and carried up a shear vein inside ductile, fine-grained galena. So one sample records an early hot event, a later deformation, and the galena flowing around the debris. A shows the same texture with bleached siltstone clasts in sphalerite-galena."},
 {"id": "L3-obs-gold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 3, "sec": "cobar", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L3/slide062_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of honey-coloured sphalerite with a red marker circle around a tiny gold grain"}], "img": "img/L3/slide062_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 3 field task · find the gold", "q": "The red circle marks visible gold at Federation. What's it sitting in, and why is that useful to know when logging?", "seen": "<p>Very low-iron, honey-coloured sphalerite with almost no sign of deformation. Gold at Federation tends to sit with this honey sphalerite and with quartz-sphalerite-galena veins, so when you're logging, honey sphalerite is a cue to look harder with a hand lens. Deposits often have localised zones of high strain or of a particular assemblage, which is why you look at every scale.</p>", "e": "Very low-iron, honey-coloured sphalerite with almost no sign of deformation. Gold at Federation tends to sit with this honey sphalerite and with quartz-sphalerite-galena veins, so when you're logging, honey sphalerite is a cue to look harder with a hand lens. Deposits often have localised zones of high strain or of a particular assemblage, which is why you look at every scale."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-greys", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "scope", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Leica research microscope with a camera mounted on top", "cap": "A · dual transmitted and reflected light"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide013_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Computer screen showing a live grey photomicrograph", "cap": "B · live camera view"}], "img": "img/L4/slide012_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · the UNSW research microscope", "q": "The screen in B looks almost entirely grey. What's the single biggest practical difficulty for beginners in reflected light?", "seen": "<p>Everything is a shade of grey, white or pale yellow, and the differences are subtle. You get used to it. Two tips: ideally work in a dark room so your eye adapts (old-school microscopists used a completely blacked-out room), and always compare a grain with its neighbours, because perceived brightness and colour depend on what's next to it. Pyrite looks bright beside sphalerite and noticeably duller beside galena.</p>", "e": "Everything is a shade of grey, white or pale yellow, and the differences are subtle. You get used to it. Two tips: ideally work in a dark room so your eye adapts (old-school microscopists used a completely blacked-out room), and always compare a grain with its neighbours, because perceived brightness and colour depend on what's next to it. Pyrite looks bright beside sphalerite and noticeably duller beside galena."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-covsph", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "props", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Covellite in PPL at no rotation: patchy pale and deep blue"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Covellite rotated 45 degrees: the blues have changed intensity and tone"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Sphalerite under crossed polars with bright yellow and orange spots inside dark grains"}], "img": "img/L4/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": true, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · covellite and sphalerite", "q": "The first two are the same covellite grains, rotated 45°. Name the property. The third is sphalerite under crossed polars. Where are those coloured spots coming from?", "seen": "<p>Covellite shows extreme <b>bireflectance pleochroism</b>: rotate the stage and the blue changes in both tone and brightness. Nothing else common looks like this, so once you've seen it you won't mistake covellite. The sphalerite shows <b>internal reflections</b>: light refracts into the semi-transparent grain, bounces off fractures and comes back as yellow, orange and red flashes. In sphalerite that's diagnostic.</p>", "e": "Covellite shows extreme bireflectance pleochroism: rotate the stage and the blue changes in both tone and brightness. Nothing else common looks like this, so once you've seen it you won't mistake covellite. The sphalerite shows internal reflections: light refracts into the semi-transparent grain, bounces off fractures and comes back as yellow, orange and red flashes. In sphalerite that's diagnostic."},
 {"id": "L4-obs-timing", "kind": "obs", "lec": 4, "sec": "textures", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L4/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "Pale yellow pyrite grains with embayed edges, surrounded by darker grey sphalerite and bright galena"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide017_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "A smooth, lobed bright galena inclusion inside grey sphalerite"}, {"src": "img/L4/slide018_1.jpg", "mic": true, "alt": "A pyrite grain with ragged, eaten-away margins filled by other sulphides"}], "img": "img/L4/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": true, "lbl": "Lecture 4 field task · reading timing from texture", "q": "For each, which mineral came first? One of them shows two minerals that grew together. Which one, and what gives it away?", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Left:</b> pyrite first, its margins eaten away, then sphalerite (darker grey), then galena (bright white). Paragenesis: pyrite, sphalerite, galena, all readable in one field of view.</li><li><b>Middle:</b> a galena inclusion in sphalerite with smooth, rounded, mutually indented (cuspate-lobate) boundaries. Neither cuts the other, so they co-crystallised: an equilibrium texture.</li><li><b>Right:</b> caries (or atoll) texture: pyrite partly dissolved and replaced by later sulphide, like a tooth with cavities.</li></ul>", "e": "Left: pyrite first, its margins eaten away, then sphalerite (darker grey), then galena (bright white). Paragenesis: pyrite, sphalerite, galena, all readable in one field of view. Middle: a galena inclusion in sphalerite with smooth, rounded, mutually indented (cuspate-lobate) boundaries. Neither cuts the other, so they co-crystallised: an equilibrium texture. Right: caries (or atoll) texture: pyrite partly dissolved and replaced by later sulphide, like a tooth with cavities."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-cast", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of dark, finely layered sheared rock with a pale band and a scale card", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide001_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale outcrop with tight, closely spaced folds", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L5/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · two outcrops", "q": "Name the structure in each, and say which would make the better fluid pathway.", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a shear zone: intensely flattened, foliated rock with a strong fabric. <b>B</b> is very tight, near-isoclinal folding of metasediments. Shear zones are the classic fluid highway, especially at the brittle-ductile transition, because repeated movement keeps reopening permeability. Fold hinges can also trap fluid, as you'll see later. Both matter for orogenic gold.</p>", "e": "A is a shear zone: intensely flattened, foliated rock with a strong fabric. B is very tight, near-isoclinal folding of metasediments. Shear zones are the classic fluid highway, especially at the brittle-ductile transition, because repeated movement keeps reopening permeability. Fold hinges can also trap fluid, as you'll see later. Both matter for orogenic gold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-bowdens", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide005_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with mixed angular and rounded clasts of several rock types", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core packed with pale clasts of varied size", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with an orange altered top and a dark fine breccia below", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide005_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Orange core cut by a network of dark filled fractures", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L5/slide005_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · breccias at Bowdens, NSW", "q": "Bowdens is an epithermal silver deposit. Use what you learned in Lecture 3: which of these is a jig-saw breccia, and which formed from fluid under high pressure?", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> is the jig-saw breccia: the orange clasts still fit together, separated by dark fill. Only fracturing and dilation. <b>A and B</b> are polymictic breccias with mixed clast types; one of these comes from an epiclastic, avalanche-style deposit off the caldera wall, where gravity did the work. <b>C</b> shows a rock-flour breccia: high-pressure hydrothermal fluid ground the rock so fine that it's turned black. Describe clasts as monomictic (one type) or polymictic (many), angular or rounded, and sorted or not.</p>", "e": "D is the jig-saw breccia: the orange clasts still fit together, separated by dark fill. Only fracturing and dilation. A and B are polymictic breccias with mixed clast types; one of these comes from an epiclastic, avalanche-style deposit off the caldera wall, where gravity did the work. C shows a rock-flour breccia: high-pressure hydrothermal fluid ground the rock so fine that it's turned black. Describe clasts as monomictic (one type) or polymictic (many), angular or rounded, and sorted or not."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-rylstone", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "cast", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Road cutting with pale and orange layered volcanic ash beds and a person for scale"}], "img": "img/L5/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Rylstone Volcanics, NSW", "q": "These are interbedded ash beds and sandstone. Why are some layers more altered and recessed than others?", "seen": "<p>The fine-grained ash beds are more altered, weathered back and fractured, while the coarser sandstone stands out. Fine ash is more reactive and the bed contacts are planes of weakness, so fluid preferentially went along and into particular layers. Bedding was a pre-existing structure that controlled where alteration went.</p>", "e": "The fine-grained ash beds are more altered, weathered back and fractured, while the coarser sandstone stands out. Fine ash is more reactive and the bed contacts are planes of weakness, so fluid preferentially went along and into particular layers. Bedding was a pre-existing structure that controlled where alteration went."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-phases", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "scale", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Five block diagrams showing rift, spreading, island arc, closing and collision phases"}], "img": "img/L5/slide022_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · a basin's life story", "q": "Match each phase to the deposits it favours: rift, spreading, island arc, closing.", "seen": "<ul><li><b>Rift:</b> little ore from the rift itself, but deep mantle-derived carbonatites can come up through continental rifts (rare earths).</li><li><b>Spreading:</b> thinned crust and decompression melting: VMS and magmatic sulphide deposits.</li><li><b>Island arc:</b> subduction magmas: porphyry and epithermal.</li><li><b>Closing:</b> collision and metamorphism: orogenic gold and many others.</li></ul><p class=\"so\">Pull the crust apart and you thin it, so magma rises by decompression and fluids find easy paths. That's why extension features in so many deposit types.</p>", "e": "Pull the crust apart and you thin it, so magma rises by decompression and fluids find easy paths. That's why extension features in so many deposit types."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-nswfaults", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "scale", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide014_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Map of central NSW with many coloured fault lines, mostly trending north-south, with named faults listed"}], "img": "img/L5/slide014_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · faults of central NSW", "q": "Most of the major faults run roughly north-south. Why, and how could you use this map to hunt for deposits?", "seen": "<p>Eastern Australia grew by accretion: terranes were added from the east onto the craton in the west, so the main sutures and faults are oriented roughly north-south. Deposits cluster where major faults intersect minor cross-faults. In the late 1970s and 1980s one Western Mining Corporation geologist, Ed O'Driscoll, built his targeting on intersecting lineaments in satellite images, and that thinking is credited as part of the path to Olympic Dam. A big deposit needs a lot of fluid, and a lot of fluid needs a big structure.</p>", "e": "Eastern Australia grew by accretion: terranes were added from the east onto the craton in the west, so the main sutures and faults are oriented roughly north-south. Deposits cluster where major faults intersect minor cross-faults. In the late 1970s and 1980s one Western Mining Corporation geologist, Ed O'Driscoll, built his targeting on intersecting lineaments in satellite images, and that thinking is credited as part of the path to Olympic Dam. A big deposit needs a lot of fluid, and a lot of fluid needs a big structure."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-extcomp", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two block diagrams: extension with meteoric water flowing down a fault into a single orebody, and compression with fluid squeezed up into several smaller orebodies"}], "img": "img/L5/slide020_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · same rocks, different stress", "q": "Compare the top (extension) and bottom (compression) diagrams. How does the ore body change shape, and why?", "seen": "<p>In extension, the fault opens a pathway, meteoric water flows down through limestone and mixes with basinal fluid, and you get one large lens on the fault. In compression the fold and fault act as an aquitard: fluid is squeezed up beneath it and replaces favourable beds, giving a series of smaller lenses. Same rocks, different stress regime, completely different ore geometry. And if there's compression somewhere in an orogen, there's extension somewhere else.</p>", "e": "In extension, the fault opens a pathway, meteoric water flows down through limestone and mixes with basinal fluid, and you get one large lens on the fault. In compression the fold and fault act as an aquitard: fluid is squeezed up beneath it and replaces favourable beds, giving a series of smaller lenses. Same rocks, different stress regime, completely different ore geometry. And if there's compression somewhere in an orogen, there's extension somewhere else."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-carlin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Diagram of Carlin-type gold: fluid rising up a fault and spreading laterally into silty limestone beds, with jasperoid and decalcification"}], "img": "img/L5/slide023_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Carlin-type gold", "q": "Why does the gold spread sideways at certain levels instead of staying on the fault?", "seen": "<p>The extensional fault is the pipe; the silty limestone beds are the sponge. Fluid rises up the fault and reacts laterally with favourable, reactive beds, dissolving carbonate (decalcification) and replacing it with silica (jasperoid). The gold is micron-sized and invisible, so you explore for the alteration and the right host beds. Carlin deposits in Nevada have produced tens of millions of ounces, and important ones are now known in southern China. Without extension, there'd be no deposit.</p>", "e": "The extensional fault is the pipe; the silty limestone beds are the sponge. Fluid rises up the fault and reacts laterally with favourable, reactive beds, dissolving carbonate (decalcification) and replacing it with silica (jasperoid). The gold is micron-sized and invisible, so you explore for the alteration and the right host beds. Carlin deposits in Nevada have produced tens of millions of ounces, and important ones are now known in southern China. Without extension, there'd be no deposit."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-enechelon", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide027_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Block diagram of strike-slip deformation with synthetic en echelon faults"}], "img": "img/L5/slide027_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · strike-slip", "q": "Strike-slip movement produces stepped, sigmoidal en echelon veins. In the Lachlan Fold Belt goldfields, are those the gold-bearing veins?", "seen": "<p>Usually not. There, the sigmoidal en echelon veins are early D1 structures, crystallised during movement (syn-deformational, rootless veins), and they're generally barren. The gold-bearing veins are much flatter and formed later. That's the timing framework again: know which vein generation carries the gold.</p>", "e": "Usually not. There, the sigmoidal en echelon veins are early D1 structures, crystallised during movement (syn-deformational, rootless veins), and they're generally barren. The gold-bearing veins are much flatter and formed later. That's the timing framework again: know which vein generation carries the gold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-zagros", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "traps", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Map of the Zagros fold-thrust belt between the Arabian and Iranian plates with oil fields along anticlines"}], "img": "img/L5/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the Zagros fold belt", "q": "Arabia pushing into Iran built a fold-thrust belt full of huge anticlines. Why don't the oil fields leak out up the many faults?", "seen": "<p>Two seals. Shale horizons in the fold hinges are impermeable, so oil rising into the anticline is trapped beneath them. And fault movement grinds rock to clay gouge, which neither oil nor gas can flow through, so many faults act as barriers rather than leaks. Structures control energy resources exactly as they control metals.</p>", "e": "Two seals. Shale horizons in the fold hinges are impermeable, so oil rising into the anticline is trapped beneath them. And fault movement grinds rock to clay gouge, which neither oil nor gas can flow through, so many faults act as barriers rather than leaks. Structures control energy resources exactly as they control metals."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-orofold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "traps", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cross-section of orogenic gold formation with metamorphic fluids rising along faults into fold hinges and saddle reefs"}], "img": "img/L5/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · orogenic gold in folds", "q": "Find the three places gold can be trapped in this compressional system.", "seen": "<p>Metamorphic fluids generated at depth (where pyrrhotite converts to pyrite, releasing arsenic, antimony and H<sub>2</sub>S) rise along faults and collect in: <b>fold hinges</b>, the zones of weakness; <b>saddle reefs</b>, where slight extension during folding opens gaps between beds at the hinge, as at Bendigo and Ballarat; and <b>replacement</b> of reactive units such as limestone or banded iron formation at the top of the fold.</p>", "e": "Metamorphic fluids generated at depth (where pyrrhotite converts to pyrite, releasing arsenic, antimony and H 2 S) rise along faults and collect in: fold hinges, the zones of weakness; saddle reefs, where slight extension during folding opens gaps between beds at the hinge, as at Bendigo and Ballarat; and replacement of reactive units such as limestone or banded iron formation at the top of the fold."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-superpit", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Aerial view of the huge terraced Superpit beside the town of Kalgoorlie", "cap": "A · the Superpit, Golden Mile"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide028_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a quartz vein in dark rock with visible gold", "cap": "B · the target"}], "img": "img/L5/slide028_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the Kalgoorlie Superpit", "q": "The pit is long and narrow, kilometres in length. What controls that shape, and what does B tell you about why visible gold is rare here?", "seen": "<p>The pit follows a structural corridor: a cluster of shear-zone-hosted lodes, so the excavation is elongate along strike. It has been mined since the 1890s, and old underground workings have been swallowed as the pit grew. B is a quartz vein with gold, but on the Golden Mile most gold is fine-grained and invisible, so a sample like B is the exception rather than the rule. Kalgoorlie is one of the richest gold fields on Earth. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording</span></p>", "e": "The pit follows a structural corridor: a cluster of shear-zone-hosted lodes, so the excavation is elongate along strike. It has been mined since the 1890s, and old underground workings have been swallowed as the pit grew. B is a quartz vein with gold, but on the Golden Mile most gold is fine-grained and invisible, so a sample like B is the exception rather than the rule. Kalgoorlie is one of the richest gold fields on Earth."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-redlake", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological map of the Red Lake greenstone belt with the Campbell-Red Lake mine marked (22.1 million ounces)", "cap": "A · map"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide037_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "3D view of the Red Lake mine zones plunging steeply with depth", "cap": "B · mine model"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide037_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with flat-lying quartz veins and a miner", "cap": "C · underground"}], "img": "img/L5/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Red Lake, Canada", "q": "Red Lake in the Superior Craton has produced about 22 million ounces. Compare the overall shape of the orebody in B with the veins in C. What's characteristic?", "seen": "<p>The deposit as a whole plunges steeply to great depth, but the individual quartz veins and lenses in C are close to horizontal. Steep plunging shoots made of flat-lying veins are very characteristic of orogenic gold. These systems go very deep, and high gold prices now make it worth mining levels that were uneconomic a few years ago, even though deep mining is expensive (you have to cool the air).</p>", "e": "The deposit as a whole plunges steeply to great depth, but the individual quartz veins and lenses in C are close to horizontal. Steep plunging shoots made of flat-lying veins are very characteristic of orogenic gold. These systems go very deep, and high gold prices now make it worth mining levels that were uneconomic a few years ago, even though deep mining is expensive (you have to cool the air)."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-granny", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geological map of the Granny Smith area with a granite, porphyry dykes, banded iron formation and mineralised structures along one side of the granite"}], "img": "img/L5/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Granny Smith, Yilgarn", "q": "The same granite and the same tectonic setting occur elsewhere in the belt with no deposit. Here, the mineralisation is all on one side of the granite margin. Why one side only?", "seen": "<p>The granite behaved as a rheological barrier: a strong, rigid body that fluid couldn't pass through, so fluid ponded and deposited gold along the structures against its margin on one side. Local structure, not the regional setting, decides whether you get a deposit, which is why two areas that look identical at regional scale can be so different. Porphyry dykes and banded iron formation also localise ore here.</p>", "e": "The granite behaved as a rheological barrier: a strong, rigid body that fluid couldn't pass through, so fluid ponded and deposited gold along the structures against its margin on one side. Local structure, not the regional setting, decides whether you get a deposit, which is why two areas that look identical at regional scale can be so different. Porphyry dykes and banded iron formation also localise ore here."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-continuum", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide033_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Schematic crustal profile showing gold deposits from sub-greenschist to granulite facies along one shear system"}], "img": "img/L5/slide033_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · the crustal continuum", "q": "One shear system, many depths. What's the idea here?", "seen": "<p>The crustal continuum model: the same kind of fluid system can deposit gold anywhere from deep, hot granulite facies up to shallow sub-greenschist facies along one connected plumbing system. The style of the deposit changes with depth (ductile shear-hosted deep, brittle veins and breccias shallow), but it's one system. The biggest Yilgarn deposits, Kalgoorlie included, sit in the upper greenschist part of the profile.</p>", "e": "The crustal continuum model: the same kind of fluid system can deposit gold anywhere from deep, hot granulite facies up to shallow sub-greenschist facies along one connected plumbing system. The style of the deposit changes with depth (ductile shear-hosted deep, brittle veins and breccias shallow), but it's one system. The biggest Yilgarn deposits, Kalgoorlie included, sit in the upper greenschist part of the profile."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-source", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "yilgarn", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide031_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two people with a very large gold-bearing specimen on a pallet"}], "img": "img/L5/slide031_3.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · where did the gold come from?", "q": "A spectacular specimen like this begs the question: where did all this gold come from?", "seen": "<p>There's no single answer. There were multiple gold events of different ages and probably multiple sources: granites, where gold concentrates in late fractionated melts and fluids; basalts, from which fluid scavenges small amounts over huge volumes and delivers it to shear zones; and an original mantle contribution. One nice proof of timing: at some nickel mines, gold turns up in the same structures but came in during a later orogenic event, long after the nickel ore formed with its host rocks.</p>", "e": "There's no single answer. There were multiple gold events of different ages and probably multiple sources: granites, where gold concentrates in late fractionated melts and fluids; basalts, from which fluid scavenges small amounts over huge volumes and delivers it to shear zones; and an original mantle contribution. One nice proof of timing: at some nickel mines, gold turns up in the same structures but came in during a later orogenic event, long after the nickel ore formed with its host rocks."},
 {"id": "L5-obs-pilbara", "kind": "obs", "lec": 5, "sec": "pilbara", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L5/slide046_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Geologist standing among tall, vertical, pencil-like rock columns", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide045_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Diagram of a diapiric dome with flattening on top and constriction on the sides", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide049_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Old mine adit tunnel cut into pale weathered rock", "cap": "C · Warrawoona"}, {"src": "img/L5/slide049_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Quartz veins in sheared rock with a pen for scale", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L5/slide046_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 5 field task · Pilbara outcrops", "q": "What are the \"pencils\" in A, and how does B explain them?", "seen": "<p>A shows vertical L-tectonites: rock stretched straight down into rods as the greenstone sank between rising domes. B shows the strain pattern of a rising dome: flattening on top, constriction on the sides, and cascading folds with potential L-tectonites in the hinge zones. The zone of sinking is also where gold turns up. C is an old working at Warrawoona: the old miners literally followed the steep shear zone. D shows the quartz veins in that sheared rock.</p>", "e": "A shows vertical L-tectonites: rock stretched straight down into rods as the greenstone sank between rising domes. B shows the strain pattern of a rising dome: flattening on top, constriction on the sides, and cascading folds with potential L-tectonites in the hinge zones. The zone of sinking is also where gold turns up. C is an old working at Warrawoona: the old miners literally followed the steep shear zone. D shows the quartz veins in that sheared rock."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-pillows", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "dive1977", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A submersible's sampling arm reaching towards a smoking chimney on the dark seafloor", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide008_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rounded, bulbous dark lava forms on the seafloor", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L6/slide007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · on the seafloor", "q": "A shows a sample being taken. B is lava on the floor of the rise. What is the lava, and what would you expect its surface to look like in hand specimen?", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is the submersible's arm snapping a sample off an active black smoker, so the mineralogy can be studied as it forms. <b>B</b> is recent, still-hot pillow lava. Lava erupting into 2 °C water chills instantly, so the rims are glassy. A sample from the active Brothers volcano shows exactly that: very glassy, because it froze the moment it met the cold water.</p>", "e": "A is the submersible's arm snapping a sample off an active black smoker, so the mineralogy can be studied as it forms. B is recent, still-hot pillow lava. Lava erupting into 2 °C water chills instantly, so the rims are glassy. A sample from the active Brothers volcano shows exactly that: very glassy, because it froze the moment it met the cold water."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-smokers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "smoker", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two vents side by side: on the left pale cloudy fluid, on the right a dense black plume"}], "img": "img/L6/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · two vents", "q": "Which vent is hotter, and what is each one putting into the water?", "seen": "<p>The right-hand vent is a <b>black smoker</b>: hotter (350 to 400 °C), with dissolved metals precipitating as tiny dark sulphide particles. The left is a <b>white smoker</b>: cooler (260 to 300 °C), giving off lighter-coloured barium, calcium and silicon minerals such as baryte, anhydrite and amorphous silica. The sulphide ore comes from the black smokers, not the white ones.</p>", "e": "The right-hand vent is a black smoker: hotter (350 to 400 °C), with dissolved metals precipitating as tiny dark sulphide particles. The left is a white smoker: cooler (260 to 300 °C), giving off lighter-coloured barium, calcium and silicon minerals such as baryte, anhydrite and amorphous silica. The sulphide ore comes from the black smokers, not the white ones."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-chimney", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "chimney", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide023_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A broken black smoker chimney fragment labelled chalcopyrite, sphalerite, pyrite and anhydrite across its thickness"}], "img": "img/L6/slide023_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · a real chimney, cut open", "q": "The fragment is labelled across its thickness. Which side faced the hot central channel, and which faced the seawater?", "seen": "<p>The <b>chalcopyrite</b> side lined the hot channel: copper minerals need the hottest fluid. Moving outwards, the minerals step down in temperature through sphalerite and pyrite to <b>anhydrite</b> on the outer wall, where heated seawater precipitated calcium sulphate. Read any chimney the same way: copper inside, zinc and iron in the middle, sulphate outside.</p>", "e": "The chalcopyrite side lined the hot channel: copper minerals need the hottest fluid. Moving outwards, the minerals step down in temperature through sphalerite and pyrite to anhydrite on the outer wall, where heated seawater precipitated calcium sulphate. Read any chimney the same way: copper inside, zinc and iron in the middle, sulphate outside."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-fauna", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "life", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White tubes with red plumes: giant tube worms", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide019_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A pale long-legged crab on dark rock", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide019_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pink shrimp crowded on pale mineral-coated rock", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L6/slide019_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · vent fauna", "q": "None of these animals can use sunlight at 2 km. What's at the base of their food chain?", "seen": "<p><b>Chemistry, not light.</b> Microbes use the energy in the vent chemicals (especially sulphide) to make organic matter. The tube worms (A) farm those microbes inside their bodies; crabs (B) and prawns (C) graze and hunt in the same food web. These organisms are extremophiles.</p>", "e": "Chemistry, not light. Microbes use the energy in the vent chemicals (especially sulphide) to make organic matter. The tube worms (A) farm those microbes inside their bodies; crabs (B) and prawns (C) graze and hunt in the same food web. These organisms are extremophiles."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-claw", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "find", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A robotic claw gripping the side of a chimney", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide026_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A claw placing a rusty chimney piece into a numbered sample tray", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L6/slide026_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · rock sampling", "q": "Why does each sample go into its own numbered bay?", "seen": "<p>The claw breaks the chimney off mechanically, then drops it into a numbered container. With the vehicle's position known, every sample is tied to an exact spot on the seafloor, so the mineralogy and chemistry can be mapped. It's expensive data: a research cruise costs several million dollars, and one towed sensor array lost after the Hunga Tonga eruption was worth about $10 million.</p>", "e": "The claw breaks the chimney off mechanically, then drops it into a numbered container. With the vehicle's position known, every sample is tied to an exact spot on the seafloor, so the mineralogy and chemistry can be mapped. It's expensive data: a research cruise costs several million dollars, and one towed sensor array lost after the Hunga Tonga eruption was worth about $10 million."},
 {"id": "L6-obs-brothers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 6, "sec": "kermadec", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L6/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Tall chimney specimen with orange-brown and grey patches", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide035_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey and dark chimney specimen", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide035_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Seafloor view of two smoking chimneys labelled twin beehives", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L6/slide036_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Several orange and grey chimneys on the seafloor at Brothers", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L6/slide035_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 6 field task · Brothers NW caldera", "q": "A and B are a copper-rich and a zinc-rich chimney from the NW caldera. C and D show chimneys in place. What different conditions do Cu-rich and Zn-rich chimneys record, and why might one chimney be both?", "seen": "<p>A <b>Cu-rich</b> chimney grew from <b>high-temperature</b> fluid (chalcopyrite forms around 350 °C); a <b>Zn-rich</b> one from <b>cooler</b> fluid (sphalerite, galena). One chimney can change along its length: in one example the side grew at high temperature while the very top was lower temperature, with sphalerite and pyrite. Activity changes all the time: in D some chimneys are active and others have been broken off and gone quiet. C is the \"twin beehives\" site, where a copper-rich chimney was sampled.</p>", "e": "A Cu-rich chimney grew from high-temperature fluid (chalcopyrite forms around 350 °C); a Zn-rich one from cooler fluid (sphalerite, galena). One chimney can change along its length: in one example the side grew at high temperature while the very top was lower temperature, with sphalerite and pyrite. Activity changes all the time: in D some chimneys are active and others have been broken off and gone quiet. C is the \"twin beehives\" site, where a copper-rich chimney was sampled."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-spinifex", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "komatiite", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Green rock with long, thin, criss-crossing dark blades"}], "img": "img/L7/slide001_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · a komatiite flow top", "q": "Name the texture. What does it tell you about how this lava cooled?", "seen": "<p>This is <b>spinifex texture</b>: long, thin, bladed crystals (originally olivine) criss-crossing like the spinifex grass of the WA outback. It forms when ultramafic lava cools very fast, in the thin quenched sheet flows and flow tops. The rock is green because metamorphism turned the olivine to serpentine, which is why the host belts are called greenstone belts.</p>", "e": "This is spinifex texture: long, thin, bladed crystals (originally olivine) criss-crossing like the spinifex grass of the WA outback. It forms when ultramafic lava cools very fast, in the thin quenched sheet flows and flow tops. The rock is green because metamorphism turned the olivine to serpentine, which is why the host belts are called greenstone belts."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-lefroy", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "kambalda", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Flat, pale blue expanse of a salt lake with small dark islands", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L7/slide026_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Aerial view of orange open-cut pits in flat red country", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L7/slide025_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · the setting", "q": "A is Lake Lefroy, a salt lake; one mine runs under it. B shows the Kambalda open cuts in the 1970s. What problem does the salt lake create for mining sulphide ore underneath it?", "seen": "<p>Salt water and oxidising sulphides wreck equipment. Down that mine, equipment lasts no more than about four years, because everything rusts and the oxidising sulphides make sulphuric acid. It makes mining under the lake very costly. Open cuts (B) were the cheap start when the ore came close to surface.</p>", "e": "Salt water and oxidising sulphides wreck equipment. Down that mine, equipment lasts no more than about four years, because everything rusts and the oxidising sulphides make sulphuric acid. It makes mining under the lake very costly. Open cuts (B) were the cheap start when the ore came close to surface."},
 {"id": "L7-obs-kamb-ore", "kind": "obs", "lec": 7, "sec": "kambalda", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L7/slide030_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall of layered pale and dark rock", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L7/slide030_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark rounded pillow shapes with bright metallic material filling the gaps, a hammer for scale", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L7/slide030_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 7 field task · underground at Kambalda", "q": "A and B are both ore. Describe how the sulphide sits in each.", "seen": "<p><b>A</b> is a banded, nickel-rich cumulate: layers with disseminated sulphides that settled out with the crystals. <b>B</b> is sulphide filling the spaces between basalt pillows: the dense sulphide liquid drained down into gaps in the footwall. Both are segregation; B shows how mobile the sulphide liquid was.</p>", "e": "A is a banded, nickel-rich cumulate: layers with disseminated sulphides that settled out with the crystals. B is sulphide filling the spaces between basalt pillows: the dense sulphide liquid drained down into gaps in the footwall. Both are segregation; B shows how mobile the sulphide liquid was."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-shatter", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "sudbury", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A hand-held rock with fine, fan-like striations across its surface"}], "img": "img/L8/slide007_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · a hand specimen from Sudbury", "q": "The striations fan across the surface. What kind of structure is this, and what does it tell you about Sudbury?", "seen": "<p>This looks like a <b>shatter cone</b>: striated, cone-shaped fracture surfaces made by the shock wave of a high-velocity impact. The beautiful shatter cones around Sudbury are part of the evidence that the basin is an impact structure. Ordinary igneous or tectonic processes don’t make them.</p>", "e": "This looks like a shatter cone: striated, cone-shaped fracture surfaces made by the shock wave of a high-velocity impact. The beautiful shatter cones around Sudbury are part of the evidence that the basin is an impact structure. Ordinary igneous or tectonic processes don’t make them."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-monche", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "norilsk", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide015_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist standing on a hill above a barren, smoky industrial landscape with a smelter", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey outcrop with pale veins and a hammer", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale weathered rock surface studded with dark, protruding crystals and a hammer", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide016_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A brassy, metallic boulder with a hammer", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L8/slide015_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Monchegorsk, Arctic Russia", "q": "In A, a geologist stands above a town where nickel has been smelted since the 1930s. In C the dark crystals stand out from a pale surface. Why, and what is D?", "seen": "<p>Decades of smelting have made Monchegorsk badly polluted: little vegetation, sulphuric acid in the air, and a freshwater lake about 50 km away with a pH of 3. The upside for a geologist is perfect outcrop, because nothing grows on it. In C, acid has dissolved the <b>plagioclase</b> along grain boundaries, so the resistant <b>orthopyroxene</b> crystals of the gabbronorite stand proud. D is <b>massive sulphide ore</b>, mainly pyrrhotite with pentlandite and chalcopyrite.</p>", "e": "Decades of smelting have made Monchegorsk badly polluted: little vegetation, sulphuric acid in the air, and a freshwater lake about 50 km away with a pH of 3. The upside for a geologist is perfect outcrop, because nothing grows on it. In C, acid has dissolved the plagioclase along grain boundaries, so the resistant orthopyroxene crystals of the gabbronorite stand proud. D is massive sulphide ore, mainly pyrrhotite with pentlandite and chalcopyrite."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-layers", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "bushveld", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of alternating black and white layers", "cap": "A · Bushveld"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide038_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Boulder with parallel dark and grey bands and a hammer", "cap": "B · Sopche, Monchegorsk"}], "img": "img/L8/slide037_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · layered rocks", "q": "Name the black and the white layers in A, and explain why they alternate.", "seen": "<p>Black is <b>chromitite</b>, white is <b>anorthosite</b>. Each new batch of magma crystallised chromite, which sank, and plagioclase, which floated: a chromitite layer, then anorthosite, then the next batch. It looks like sedimentary bedding, and the process is just as physical. B is layered chromitite at Sopche, more like a layered intrusion than a pod.</p>", "e": "Black is chromitite, white is anorthosite. Each new batch of magma crystallised chromite, which sank, and plagioclase, which floated: a chromitite layer, then anorthosite, then the next batch. It looks like sedimentary bedding, and the process is just as physical. B is layered chromitite at Sopche, more like a layered intrusion than a pod."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-coolac", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "podiform", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grassy hills of the Coolac Serpentinite Belt", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Weathered rock with parallel raised ribs", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale rock with a dark vein-like streak and a red pen", "cap": "C"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide039_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale rock studded with dark rounded nodules and a hammer", "cap": "D"}], "img": "img/L8/slide039_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Coolac Serpentinite Belt, southern NSW", "q": "Name the chromite texture in D, and what C and B show.", "seen": "<p><b>D</b> is <b>nodular chromitite</b>: rounded chromite nodules in pale dunite, typical of podiform deposits. <b>C</b> is a vein-like segregation of chromitite. <b>B</b> is primary layering in the host harzburgite, alternating olivine-rich and orthopyroxene-rich layers that weather out as ribs. A is the landscape of the serpentinite belt.</p>", "e": "D is nodular chromitite: rounded chromite nodules in pale dunite, typical of podiform deposits. C is a vein-like segregation of chromitite. B is primary layering in the host harzburgite, alternating olivine-rich and orthopyroxene-rich layers that weather out as ribs. A is the landscape of the serpentinite belt."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-khibiny", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "phos", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Large grey open-cut mine in treeless hills", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide042_3.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "White rock with sprays of dark needle-like crystals", "cap": "B"}, {"src": "img/L8/slide042_4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rock with golden-brown radiating bladed crystals labelled astrophyllite", "cap": "C"}], "img": "img/L8/slide042_2.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Khibiny massif, Arctic Circle", "q": "A is an apatite mine. Why would the rocks of B and C tell you this is an alkaline intrusion?", "seen": "<p>Khibiny is a circular, highly alkaline intrusion, rich in sodium, potassium, fluorine and CO₂. Its minerals show it: dark sprays of Na-rich pyroxene and amphibole (B) and golden <b>astrophyllite</b> (C), a Ti-bearing alkaline silicate. Ordinary gabbros and granites don’t carry these. The open cut (A) mines apatite, with rare earths as a by-product.</p>", "e": "Khibiny is a circular, highly alkaline intrusion, rich in sodium, potassium, fluorine and CO₂. Its minerals show it: dark sprays of Na-rich pyroxene and amphibole (B) and golden astrophyllite (C), a Ti-bearing alkaline silicate. Ordinary gabbros and granites don’t carry these. The open cut (A) mines apatite, with rare earths as a by-product."},
 {"id": "L8-obs-shev", "kind": "obs", "lec": 8, "sec": "laterite", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L8/slide044_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Orange and brown pit wall of weathered serpentinite labelled with nickel grades of 1.88, 1.80 and 1.47 percent"}], "img": "img/L8/slide044_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 8 field task · Shevchenko deposit, Urals", "q": "The face is weathered serpentinite with grades of 1.47 to 1.88% Ni. What was this rock before weathering, and why is it now ore?", "seen": "<p>It was an <b>ultramafic rock</b>, serpentinised peridotite or dunite, carrying only about 0.2 to 0.4% Ni in its olivine. Intense weathering stripped out the magnesium and silica and left the nickel behind, concentrated several times over, sorbed onto fine goethite and in clays. No sulphide was ever involved.</p>", "e": "It was an ultramafic rock, serpentinised peridotite or dunite, carrying only about 0.2 to 0.4% Ni in its olivine. Intense weathering stripped out the magnesium and silica and left the nickel behind, concentrated several times over, sorbed onto fine goethite and in clays. No sulphide was ever involved."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-slab", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/bedded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of finely layered green-grey rock with pale and dark bands, one thicker speckled grey band across the middle, 2 cm scale bar"}], "img": "img/L9/bedded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a slab of SEDEX ore", "q": "Before you read anything: describe the layering. How thick are the layers, how continuous, and are they all the same kind of material?", "seen": "<ul><li>Millimetre-scale layers, very flat and continuous across the 10 cm slab, with only gentle wavy disruption.</li><li>Three kinds of band: thin pale layers, thin dark layers, and one thicker, speckled grey band with a sharp base that fines upward.</li><li>No veins cutting the layers, no breccia, no massive sulphide lump.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is sediment that was laid down layer by layer. The thick graded band is a turbidite, the dark layers are black shale, and the thin layers between them are sphalerite and galena. The sulphides were deposited along with the mud, layer by layer, which is the core observation behind the syngenetic model.</p>", "e": "So this is sediment that was laid down layer by layer. The thick graded band is a turbidite, the dark layers are black shale, and the thin layers between them are sphalerite and galena. The sulphides were deposited along with the mud, layer by layer, which is the core observation behind the syngenetic model."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-core", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "mcarthur", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/core.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rows of shiny dark grey to black drill core with fine, slightly wavy laminations, a few broken intervals and one pale buff section"}], "img": "img/L9/core.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · drill core of the host rock", "q": "What colour is the rock, what’s its grain size, and what does that tell you about the water it was deposited in?", "seen": "<ul><li>Black to dark grey, very fine grained, finely and continuously laminated, with no sign of burrows churning the layers.</li><li>One short pale interval, probably a carbonate-richer bed.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the classic host: organic-rich dolomitic siltstone. Black means organic carbon survived, fine laminae that no animal disturbed (non-bioturbated) mean the bottom water had no oxygen. That’s the anoxic, H₂S-bearing basin floor the recipe demands. Almost every SEDEX deposit sits in rock like this. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 45</span></p>", "e": "So this is the classic host: organic-rich dolomitic siltstone. Black means organic carbon survived, fine laminae that no animal disturbed (non-bioturbated) mean the bottom water had no oxygen. That’s the anoxic, H₂S-bearing basin floor the recipe demands. Almost every SEDEX deposit sits in rock like this."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-redbed", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "chem", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/redbed.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Outcrop of red-brown, finely bedded sandstone with a geological hammer for scale", "cap": "A · outcrop, hammer for scale"}, {"src": "img/L9/hemqtz.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section of rounded clear quartz grains, each outlined by a thin dark rim, with dark material between grains", "cap": "B · thin section of the same rock"}], "img": "img/L9/redbed.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · the aquifer in the McArthur Basin", "q": "What gives the sandstone its colour, and what does that tell you about the brine that moved through it?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: red-brown, well-bedded sandstone.</li><li>B: rounded quartz grains, each with a thin dark coating; the coating is hematite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the colour is hematite, oxidised iron, coating every grain. Brine that moved through this aquifer was oxidised, which fits the north Australian pattern. In a reduced basin like Sullivan’s, the grains would be coated with pyrite instead.</p>", "e": "So the colour is hematite, oxidised iron, coating every grain. Brine that moved through this aquifer was oxidised, which fits the north Australian pattern. In a reduced basin like Sullivan’s, the grains would be coated with pyrite instead."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-micro", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "layers", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/sphlayer.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section with a pale vertical band of fine grains between dark fine-grained bands, and rounded pale clusters scattered in the dark band to its right", "cap": "A · thin section"}, {"src": "img/L9/micro830.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Thin section with dark fine matrix and wavy pale bands, one band thickened over a lump, 830 micrometre scale bar", "cap": "B · thin section, scale bar 830 µm"}], "img": "img/L9/sphlayer.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · under the microscope", "q": "At this scale, does the ore look like veins cutting the rock, or layers in it? How thick are the pale bands?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: a band of fine sphalerite grains parallel to the layering, with rounded sphalerite aggregates scattered in the darker sediment beside it.</li><li>B: pale sulphide bands a fraction of a millimetre thick between dark carbonaceous mud layers, gently draped over a lump.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the sulphide is layered with the sediment, not cutting it: microlaminae of sulphide interbedded with carbonaceous mudstone. The rounded aggregates match sphalerite clumps that grew in the water or brine and sank. Look at how fine everything is, and you can see why these ores are hard to process.</p>", "e": "So the sulphide is layered with the sediment, not cutting it: microlaminae of sulphide interbedded with carbonaceous mudstone. The rounded aggregates match sphalerite clumps that grew in the water or brine and sank. Look at how fine everything is, and you can see why these ores are hard to process."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-breccia", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "anatomy", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab: the lower third is finely laminated grey and pale ore, the upper part is a breccia of angular to rounded clasts of many colours in a dark matrix, fining up into dark sand; a thin white vein cuts both; 2 cm scale"}], "img": "img/L9/breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a slab from near a vent", "q": "Describe the two parts of this slab and the contact between them. What happened, in order?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bottom: finely laminated ore, the layers gently wavy, with an irregular, slightly scoured top.</li><li>Top: a sedimentary breccia of angular to rounded clasts of different rocks, fining upward into dark sand.</li><li>A thin white vein cuts both, so it came last.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So laminated Zn-Pb ore formed first, then a debris flow, probably shed off an active fault scarp, dumped breccia on top of it, and later a vein cut through everything. Breccia over banded sulphide is unusual, and breccias like this mark the vent area, where fault movement keeps shedding debris. Mineralising fluid passing through a breccia near a vent will mineralise it too.</p>", "e": "So laminated Zn-Pb ore formed first, then a debris flow, probably shed off an active fault scarp, dumped breccia on top of it, and later a vein cut through everything. Breccia over banded sulphide is unusual, and breccias like this mark the vent area, where fault movement keeps shedding debris. Mineralising fluid passing through a breccia near a vent will mineralise it too."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-isa", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "halo", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/rl1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of wavy, very fine dark and white laminae with a white band and grey patches below, 1 mm scale bar", "cap": "A · reflected light, scale bar 1 mm"}, {"src": "img/L9/rl2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of a row of pale cream euhedral grains in a pale bluish-grey matrix between dark fine-grained laminae, 1 mm scale bar", "cap": "B · reflected light, scale bar 1 mm"}], "img": "img/L9/rl1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · Mount Isa ore in reflected light", "q": "In B, the pale cream grains have straight edges and look pitted. Which mineral are they, and what about A tells you this is SEDEX ore?", "seen": "<ul><li>B: pale cream, high reflectance, hard (they stand out and are pitted from polishing), with crystal faces: <b>pyrite</b> euhedra, in a paler bluish-grey sulphide that is most likely galena.</li><li>A: very fine, wavy laminae of sulphide and dark carbonaceous sediment at the millimetre scale, gently folded.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So you have fine-grained laminated sulphide with pyrite in carbonaceous sediment, the SEDEX signature. The galena call in B is from colour and reflectance only, so treat it as likely rather than certain. The gentle folds in A could be soft-sediment folds or compaction rather than later deformation.</p>", "e": "So you have fine-grained laminated sulphide with pyrite in carbonaceous sediment, the SEDEX signature. The galena call in B is from colour and reflectance only, so treat it as likely rather than certain. The gentle folds in A could be soft-sediment folds or compaction rather than later deformation."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-galena", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "halo", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/rl4.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of a bright white mineral with sharp black triangular pits, enclosing a cream-coloured grain, set in grey material, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L9/rl4.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · a second north Australian ore", "q": "Look at the bright white mineral. What are the black triangles, and what does that identify?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright white, high reflectance, soft-looking surface.</li><li>Sharp black triangular pits.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>galena</b>: the triangles are cleavage pits, where cubic cleavage fragments plucked out during polishing. The grey around it, with lower reflectance, is most likely sphalerite and carbonate gangue. The cream grain inside isn’t identified on the slide; from colour alone it could be pyrite.</p>", "e": "So it’s galena: the triangles are cleavage pits, where cubic cleavage fragments plucked out during polishing. The grey around it, with lower reflectance, is most likely sphalerite and carbonate gangue. The cream grain inside isn’t identified on the slide; from colour alone it could be pyrite."},
 {"id": "L9-obs-hemi", "kind": "obs", "lec": 9, "sec": "world", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L9/hemimorphite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of brown, weathered, cavernous outcrop with white crusts of bladed crystals, and a dark lizard on the right"}], "img": "img/L9/hemimorphite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 9 field task · the HYC discovery outcrop", "q": "This weathered outcrop was the only surface sign of HYC. What is the white mineral, and why does it matter?", "seen": "<ul><li>Brown, iron-stained, cavernous rock: a small gossan.</li><li>White crusts of fine bladed crystals.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the white mineral is hemimorphite, Zn₄Si₂O₇(OH)₂·H₂O, a zinc silicate that forms when zinc sulphide weathers (the supergene story of Lecture 2). A tiny gossan with a little hemimorphite was all that showed at surface of what became Australia’s biggest SEDEX deposit. Syd Carter and Ron Beresford recognised it. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 46</span></p>", "e": "So the white mineral is hemimorphite, Zn₄Si₂O₇(OH)₂·H₂O, a zinc silicate that forms when zinc sulphide weathers (the supergene story of Lecture 2). A tiny gossan with a little hemimorphite was all that showed at surface of what became Australia’s biggest SEDEX deposit. Syd Carter and Ron Beresford recognised it."},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-0", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-felsic.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 0, "e": "Flattened felsic clasts in the footwall. (slide 50)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-1", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-hematite.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 1, "e": "Hydrothermal hematite flooding felsic clasts near ore: oxidised fluid, not weathering. (slide 51)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-2", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-stringer.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 2, "e": "Chalcopyrite-rich stringer ore in basalt, below the main lode. (slide 57)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-3", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-shale.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 3, "e": "Graphitic black shale with pyrite: soft and unstable to mine through. (slide 55)"},
 {"id": "L10-dcCards-4", "kind": "core", "lec": 10, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L10/ug-shalepillow.jpg", "lbl": "Decline face", "q": "What is this?", "o": ["Footwall felsic volcanics", "Hematite-flooded felsic clasts", "Chalcopyrite stringer zone", "Hanging-wall graphitic black shale", "Black shale over pillow basalt"], "a": 4, "e": "Black shale overlying pillowed basalts. (slide 52)"},
 {"id": "L10-obs-hosts", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "settings", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/basalt.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark, very fine-grained rock with a few small white spots", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/L10/rhyolite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale cream, porous, fine-grained rock with many small holes", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/L10/basalt.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · two host rocks", "q": "Describe colour, grain size and texture. Which one would host VMS ore at a mid-ocean ridge, and which in an island arc?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: dark, very fine grained, a few small amygdales or phenocrysts. A mafic volcanic: <b>basalt</b>.</li><li>B: pale, fine grained, full of small vesicles. A felsic volcanic: <b>rhyolite</b>, here pumiceous.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is the host at spreading ridges and rifts, B the host in arcs and back arcs, where the biggest deposits are. In Australia most VMS deposits have rhyolites in the footwall and basalts in the hanging wall. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So A is the host at spreading ridges and rifts, B the host in arcs and back arcs, where the biggest deposits are. In Australia most VMS deposits have rhyolites in the footwall and basalts in the hanging wall."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-culode", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/ug-culode.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall with rounded, dark green pillow shapes, some pale and bleached, with sulphide between them, under mesh"}], "img": "img/L10/ug-culode.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · a copper-rich lode in the decline", "q": "The pillow shapes are still there. What has happened to them, and why is that more intense in some places than others?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pillow outlines are preserved, but some pillows are bleached pale and others are dark green.</li><li>Sulphide fills between and replaces parts of the pillows.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So hydrothermal fluid has replaced the basalt. Where fluid flow was most intense, the pillows are bleached and replaced by chalcopyrite and pyrite; where less fluid passed, you just get strong chlorite around the pillow rims. The intensity maps the old fluid pathways. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 46</span></p>", "e": "So hydrothermal fluid has replaced the basalt. Where fluid flow was most intense, the pillows are bleached and replaced by chalcopyrite and pyrite; where less fluid passed, you just get strong chlorite around the pillow rims. The intensity maps the old fluid pathways."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-pillows", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "styles", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/pillows.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A person standing on a hillside made entirely of stacked rounded and tube-shaped lava bodies"}], "img": "img/L10/pillows.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · an ophiolite outcrop", "q": "What are these rounded shapes, and what do they tell you about where this rock formed?", "seen": "<ul><li>Stacked, rounded to tube-shaped bodies about a metre across, each with a smooth skin, packed together with the gaps between them filled.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So these are <b>pillow lavas</b>: basalt erupted underwater, where each blob quenches a skin and the next one buds off. They tell you this was the seafloor. These are in Oman, part of an ophiolite, oceanic crust shoved up onto the continent. That’s the home of Cyprus-type deposits. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 34</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So these are pillow lavas: basalt erupted underwater, where each blob quenches a skin and the next one buds off. They tell you this was the seafloor. These are in Oman, part of an ophiolite, oceanic crust shoved up onto the continent. That’s the home of Cyprus-type deposits."},
 {"id": "L10-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 10, "sec": "jaguar", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L10/ug-banded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close view of a wall of grey sulphide with wavy pale and darker bands, a lens cap for scale"}], "img": "img/L10/ug-banded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 10 field task · the main lode", "q": "From a distance this face looks like one massive block. Up close, what do you see, and what might the bands record?", "seen": "<ul><li>Wavy, contorted bands of slightly different colour and texture through the sulphide.</li><li>Very little galena: this ore is mostly pyrite, sphalerite and chalcopyrite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the “massive” sulphide is banded: layers richer in chalcopyrite, sphalerite or pyrite. Bands like these fit pulses of hotter and cooler fluid overprinting each other, later deformed. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 58 and 61</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 6 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the “massive” sulphide is banded: layers richer in chalcopyrite, sphalerite or pyrite. Bands like these fit pulses of hotter and cooler fluid overprinting each other, later deformed."},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-0", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/avein.jpg", "lbl": "Drill core", "q": "Irregular grey quartz veins with pink-orange margins. Which vein type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 1, "e": "A vein: sugary quartz with K-feldspar on its margins. (slide 26)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-1", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/bvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "A straight, continuous vein with a centre line and no halo. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 2, "e": "B vein: planar, with quartz grown in from both walls to a centre line; these carry most of the ore. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-2", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/dvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Dark sulphide-rich veins in pale, bleached rock where the feldspar is gone. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 3, "e": "D vein: pyrite-rich with a sericite halo that destroys feldspar. (slide 28)"},
 {"id": "L11-vnCards-3", "kind": "core", "lec": 11, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L11/ebvein.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Thin dark veinlets of a black mica with almost no quartz or sulphide. Which type?", "o": ["EB vein", "A vein", "B vein", "D vein"], "a": 0, "e": "EB vein: early biotite, formed deep. (slide 29)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-porph", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "host", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/granodiorite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of an evenly speckled pink, white and black coarse-grained rock", "cap": "A · hand specimen"}, {"src": "img/L11/porphyry.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a rock with larger pale pink rectangular crystals scattered through a fine-grained red, grey and black groundmass, one crystal labelled phenocryst", "cap": "B · hand specimen"}], "img": "img/L11/granodiorite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · what makes it a porphyry?", "q": "Compare the grain size in A and B. Which texture gives porphyry deposits their name, and what does it tell you about how that magma cooled?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: evenly coarse grained, all crystals about the same size: pink K-feldspar, white plagioclase and quartz, black biotite or hornblende. An equigranular granitoid such as granodiorite.</li><li>B: large, well-formed feldspar crystals (phenocrysts) in a much finer groundmass.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So B shows <b>porphyritic</b> texture, which gives the deposits their name. Two cooling rates: crystals grew slowly at depth, then the magma rose to a shallow level and the rest crystallised fast. That shallow, high-level emplacement is exactly where porphyry deposits form.</p>", "e": "So B shows porphyritic texture, which gives the deposits their name. Two cooling rates: crystals grew slowly at depth, then the magma rose to a shallow level and the rest crystallised fast. That shallow, high-level emplacement is exactly where porphyry deposits form."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-potassic", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Four photos A to D: a dark rock cut by quartz veins with K-feldspar labelled along their margins; a pink rock with dark green patches labelled biotite; a green rock with dark veins; a purple-grey rock with a network of thin veins"}], "img": "img/L11/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · potassic alteration", "q": "In A and B, what colour is the K-feldspar, and what dark mineral is growing in B? Why does that colour hint at an oxidised magma?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: K-feldspar (Kfs) alteration along the margins of quartz veins, at Bethsaida, British Columbia.</li><li>B: pervasive pink K-feldspar with patches of fine hydrothermal biotite (Bt), with quartz-molybdenite veinlets, at Red Mountain, Yukon.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is potassic alteration, always present in the core. The K-feldspar is strongly pink because it holds iron, a sign of an oxidised system. In many porphyries you won’t see much K-feldspar; secondary biotite is the commoner sign of potassic alteration. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 52</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So this is potassic alteration, always present in the core. The K-feldspar is strongly pink because it holds iron, a sign of an oxidised system. In many porphyries you won’t see much K-feldspar; secondary biotite is the commoner sign of potassic alteration."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-propylitic", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "alteration", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six photos: four green-grey drill cores, one with crosscutting veins and one with brassy grains, an outcrop of green-grey andesite with a hammer, and a close-up of a yellow-green radiating mineral in a clast"}], "img": "img/L11/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · the outer halo", "q": "Look at f, the yellow-green radiating mineral. What is it, and which alteration zone has it put you in?", "seen": "<ul><li>Cores and outcrop are green-grey: chlorite.</li><li>f: a coarse, radiating, pistachio-green mass forming a nodule in an altered volcanic clast: epidote.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So you’re in the <b>propylitic</b> zone: chlorite, epidote, albite, calcite, with magnetite or pyrite. It is always present and marks the outer halo; the first epidote marks the start of the propylitic zone. These are from Schaft Creek, BC. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 53</span> (Scott et al., 2008)</p>", "e": "So you’re in the propylitic zone: chlorite, epidote, albite, calcite, with magnetite or pyrite. It is always present and marks the outer halo; the first epidote marks the start of the propylitic zone. These are from Schaft Creek, BC. (Scott et al., 2008)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-ust", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "vectors", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/ust-hs.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six cut slabs: wavy pink-orange and grey bands; contorted black tourmaline and white quartz bands; green and lilac patches with arrows; orange zigzag bands in grey; stacked pale bands with grey comb crystals growing from one side; and a finely mottled pale rock"}], "img": "img/L11/ust-hs.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · which way is up?", "q": "In e (Umnu Hutul), the grey quartz crystals grow from one side of each band. Which way was the top of the intrusion?", "seen": "<ul><li>e: repeated pale bands, each with a flat side and grey quartz combs whose points all face the same way.</li><li>a, b, d: wavy to contorted banding; b is tourmalinised.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So USTs grow <b>downward</b> into the magma from the roof: the flat side each layer grows from is up, toward the cupola, and the crystal tips point down. The straight and wavy types make that easy to read; contorted or brecciated ones are much harder. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 33 to 35</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So USTs grow downward into the magma from the roof: the flat side each layer grows from is up, toward the cupola, and the crystal tips point down. The straight and wavy types make that easy to read; contorted or brecciated ones are much harder."},
 {"id": "L11-obs-aeromag", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "vectors", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/aeromag.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Colourful aeromagnetic map with red highs and blue-purple lows, a north-south fault line and small circles marking deposits"}], "img": "img/L11/aeromag.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · an aeromagnetic map", "q": "Chuquicamata and El Abra both sit on magnetic lows. Porphyry magmas are oxidised and rich in magnetite, so why a low?", "seen": "<ul><li>Reds are magnetic highs, blues and purples lows; the deposits (circles) sit along a fault on lows.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So hydrothermal fluid passing through destroyed the primary magnetite and demagnetised the rock. You want a porphyry that started magnetite-rich and was then flushed by lots of fluid, because the fluid brought the metal. A magnetic low inside a magnetic intrusion is a target. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 21</span> (Behn et al., 2001) <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So hydrothermal fluid passing through destroyed the primary magnetite and demagnetised the rock. You want a porphyry that started magnetite-rich and was then flushed by lots of fluid, because the fluid brought the metal. A magnetic low inside a magnetic intrusion is a target. (Behn et al., 2001)"},
 {"id": "L11-obs-climax", "kind": "obs", "lec": 11, "sec": "moly", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L11/climaxore.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Small pale grey rock with dark grey streaks and patches and a thin grey vein cutting it"}], "img": "img/L11/climaxore.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 11 field task · Climax ore", "q": "This is ore. What would you look for to see the molybdenum?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale, quartz-rich rock (silicified rhyolite porphyry) with dark grey streaks and a thin quartz vein.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So look for soft, lead-grey to bluish, platy <b>molybdenite</b> in the veinlets and disseminated through the rock. Ore is mostly stockwork veins with minor disseminations, at only 0.1 to 0.45% MoS₂, so you won’t see much. Tonnages run from 50 to 1500 Mt, mined by open cut, stoping and block caving, mostly in western North America (and now Tibet). <span class=\"srcs\">slide 40</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 9 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So look for soft, lead-grey to bluish, platy molybdenite in the veinlets and disseminated through the rock. Ore is mostly stockwork veins with minor disseminations, at only 0.1 to 0.45% MoS₂, so you won’t see much. Tonnages run from 50 to 1500 Mt, mined by open cut, stoping and block caving, mostly in western North America (and now Tibet)."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-wire", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "why", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/goldwire.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale yellow, wiry, branching gold on a grey quartz vein surface, a pen for scale"}], "img": "img/L12/goldwire.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a museum specimen", "q": "This is gold wire in quartz. What shape are the gold “wires” really built from, and what does the pale colour suggest?", "seen": "<ul><li>Fine, branching, wire-like gold on a quartz vein: crystalline gold, typical of epithermal systems.</li><li>A pale, slightly greenish yellow rather than deep yellow.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the wires are actually <b>elongated, stacked cubes</b> of gold. The colour is a rough guide to composition: pale gold is high in silver, deep yellow gold is purer. Don’t expect to see this in a real ore: most gold is invisible. <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 12 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the wires are actually elongated, stacked cubes of gold. The colour is a rough guide to composition: pale gold is high in silver, deep yellow gold is purer. Don’t expect to see this in a real ore: most gold is invisible."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-spring", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "fluids", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/hotspring.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Steaming turquoise hot pool rimmed with orange and red-brown crusts, forest behind"}], "img": "img/L12/hotspring.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a hot spring", "q": "What is this pool the surface expression of, and what might the orange crust around it be carrying?", "seen": "<ul><li>A steaming, clear turquoise pool: hot, silica-rich water.</li><li>Bright orange to red-brown crusts on the rim.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the top of an active epithermal (geothermal) system. The orange is arsenic and antimony sulphide precipitate, and in some New Zealand pools these crusts carry hundreds of grams per tonne of gold. The Broadlands geothermal field precipitates kilograms of gold a year inside its pipes. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 16</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 12 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So this is the top of an active epithermal (geothermal) system. The orange is arsenic and antimony sulphide precipitate, and in some New Zealand pools these crusts carry hundreds of grams per tonne of gold. The Broadlands geothermal field precipitates kilograms of gold a year inside its pipes."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-hera", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "mineralogy", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/hera-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Polished dark grey and brown-black rock with pale veinlets, small bright yellow specks scattered along a dark band, a cm scale card above"}], "img": "img/L12/hera-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · find the gold", "q": "Look along the dark brown band. What are the yellow specks, and how big are they?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright, metallic, butter-yellow specks a few millimetres long, concentrated along a dark brown sulphide-rich band, with thin pale veinlets cutting the grey host.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So that’s <b>visible gold</b> in polymetallic ore from the Hera mine, central NSW. Gold is the only common mineral that is this yellow, very soft and doesn’t tarnish; pyrite and chalcopyrite are paler or more brassy and much harder. Specks like these are rare in most ores, where the gold is invisible. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 36</span></p>", "e": "So that’s visible gold in polymetallic ore from the Hera mine, central NSW. Gold is the only common mineral that is this yellow, very soft and doesn’t tarnish; pyrite and chalcopyrite are paler or more brassy and much harder. Specks like these are rare in most ores, where the gold is invisible."},
 {"id": "L12-obs-reef", "kind": "obs", "lec": 12, "sec": "wits", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L12/wits-outcrop.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Rusty orange-brown outcrop of a rock packed with rounded pale pebbles in a finer matrix"}], "img": "img/L12/wits-outcrop.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 12 field task · a reef outcrop", "q": "Describe the clasts: shape, sorting, composition. What kind of rock is this, and what deposited it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Well-rounded pebbles, fairly similar in size, mostly pale vein quartz, closely packed in a finer, iron-stained matrix.</li><li>Faint layering, suggesting bedding.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s a quartz-pebble <b>conglomerate</b>: rounded clasts mean long river transport. The pebbles are almost all one kind (monomictic: vein quartz, chert, pyrite), and that’s odd, because the basin sits on Archean granite and greenstone, yet no granite or greenstone clasts appear. Gold and uraninite sit in the matrix. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 41 to 45</span></p>", "e": "So it’s a quartz-pebble conglomerate: rounded clasts mean long river transport. The pebbles are almost all one kind (monomictic: vein quartz, chert, pyrite), and that’s odd, because the basin sits on Archean granite and greenstone, yet no granite or greenstone clasts appear. Gold and uraninite sit in the matrix."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-stives", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/stives.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of white and grey quartz vein with a broad patch of yellow metallic grains, a hand holding a hammer for scale"}], "img": "img/L13/stives.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · a quartz vein underground", "q": "Everything yellow here is gold. What is it sitting in, and how would an orogenic gold geologist describe this ore?", "seen": "<ul><li>Milky white quartz with dark, sheared wall rock on either side.</li><li>A dense swarm of free, visible gold grains in the quartz.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the classic orogenic ore: <b>free gold in a quartz vein</b> along a structure, here at St Ives, Western Australia. Extremely rich, and very unusual: most orogenic ore is low grade, with gold invisible in the vein and the altered wall rock. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 1</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So this is the classic orogenic ore: free gold in a quartz vein along a structure, here at St Ives, Western Australia. Extremely rich, and very unusual: most orogenic ore is low grade, with gold invisible in the vein and the altered wall rock."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-holtermann", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "hillend", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/holtermann.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Old photograph of a bearded man in a waistcoat standing beside a tall, slab-like specimen almost as high as his chest", "cap": "A · 1872"}, {"src": "img/L13/holtermann-cast.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A man beside a life-size gold-coloured cast of a large, lumpy specimen", "cap": "B · a life-size cast"}], "img": "img/L13/holtermann.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · the biggest “nugget”", "q": "Holtermann’s “nugget” held 3000 oz of gold. Why do geologists insist it wasn’t really a nugget?", "seen": "<ul><li>A slab about as tall as a man, irregular and blocky, not rounded or water-worn.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s not a <b>nugget</b> (a detrital lump from a placer) but a piece of <b>quartz vein riddled with pure gold</b>, found on 19 October 1872 at Hawkins Hill, Hill End. At 3000 oz (93.31 kg) it’s the largest single mass of gold ever recorded, and would be worth at least US$15 million today. Bernhardt Holtermann was a storekeeper; on the goldfields the storekeepers made the money, and his fortune paid for a photographic record of the NSW goldfields that still survives. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 9</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So it’s not a nugget (a detrital lump from a placer) but a piece of quartz vein riddled with pure gold, found on 19 October 1872 at Hawkins Hill, Hill End. At 3000 oz (93.31 kg) it’s the largest single mass of gold ever recorded, and would be worth at least US$15 million today. Bernhardt Holtermann was a storekeeper; on the goldfields the storekeepers made the money, and his fortune paid for a photographic record of the NSW goldfields that still survives."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-stawell", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "stawell", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/stawell-vein.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face of black carbonaceous shale with many thin white quartz veins, cut by a thick white quartz vein"}], "img": "img/L13/stawell-vein.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · two generations of quartz", "q": "There are at least two sets of quartz here. Which came first, and which is likely to carry the gold?", "seen": "<ul><li>Many thin, irregular, deformed white quartz veins in black carbonaceous shale.</li><li>A thicker quartz vein cutting straight through them.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the thin, deformed veins are earlier and the thick vein that cuts them is later: crosscutting relationships. This is the mineralised vein of the Hanging Wall lode cutting earlier barren quartz. In orogenic gold the payable vein is usually a late one, so knowing which generation you’re looking at matters. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 25</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the thin, deformed veins are earlier and the thick vein that cuts them is later: crosscutting relationships. This is the mineralised vein of the Hanging Wall lode cutting earlier barren quartz. In orogenic gold the payable vein is usually a late one, so knowing which generation you’re looking at matters."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-carlin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "carlin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/carlin-ore.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark grey, fine-grained rock with a rough surface and a few tiny glints"}], "img": "img/L13/carlin-ore.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · Carlin ore", "q": "This is gold ore. Find the gold.", "seen": "<ul><li>A dark, fine-grained carbonaceous sedimentary rock with a little fine pyrite. No visible gold at all.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">You can’t, and that’s the point. Carlin gold is so fine it’s essentially invisible, locked in arsenian pyrite. What you can see are the arsenic and antimony minerals that come with it. Because the ore looks like waste, Carlin mines have to assay constantly to know they’re still in ore. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 31 and 33</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "You can’t, and that’s the point. Carlin gold is so fine it’s essentially invisible, locked in arsenian pyrite. What you can see are the arsenic and antimony minerals that come with it. Because the ore looks like waste, Carlin mines have to assay constantly to know they’re still in ore."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-pyrite", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "carlin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/carlin-pyrite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Electron image of a cluster of fine pyrite among quartz crystals; a zoned pyrite grain with a speckled rim around a cleaner core and an arrow across it; and a graph of element counts along the arrow, higher in the rim"}], "img": "img/L13/carlin-pyrite.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · where is the gold in the pyrite?", "q": "The laser traced across the zoned pyrite from rim to core. Where are gold and arsenic highest?", "seen": "<ul><li>A clean core and a speckled, inclusion-rich rim.</li><li>On the trace, gold, arsenic and the other trace elements jump up as the beam enters the rim and are higher across the rim than in the core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the gold sits in the <b>rim</b>: thin, arsenic-rich ore-stage pyrite grown over earlier gold-poor pyrite, when the ore fluid cooled and mixed with heated meteoric water. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 34</span> (Muntean et al., 2011) <span class=\"srcs\">slide notes</span> <span style=\"color:var(--muted)\">The recording describes the gold as being in the core; the figure and the slide notes put it in the rim, so the page follows the slide.</span></p>", "e": "So the gold sits in the rim: thin, arsenic-rich ore-stage pyrite grown over earlier gold-poor pyrite, when the ore fluid cooled and mixed with heated meteoric water. (Muntean et al., 2011) The recording describes the gold as being in the core; the figure and the slide notes put it in the rim, so the page follows the slide."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-tuena", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "tuena", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/tuena-veins.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground wall of grey-green schistose rock with pale, folded and broken quartz veins along the foliation"}], "img": "img/L13/tuena-veins.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · veins in the adit", "q": "These pale quartz veins lie along the foliation and are folded. Were they there before or after the main deformation?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale quartz in pods and lenses, folded and dragged into the foliation, some rootless.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So these veins were there <b>before</b> or during deformation: early, refolded veins and syn-kinematic veins. They’re not the gold veins. At Tuena, as across the Lachlan, the gold is in late, <b>post-kinematic</b>, planar, carbonate-rich veins that cut the foliation. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 39 and 40</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 13 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So these veins were there before or during deformation: early, refolded veins and syn-kinematic veins. They’re not the gold veins. At Tuena, as across the Lachlan, the gold is in late, post-kinematic, planar, carbonate-rich veins that cut the foliation."},
 {"id": "L13-obs-tuenagold", "kind": "obs", "lec": 13, "sec": "tuena", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L13/tuena-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view of grey matrix with thin dark fractures, along which small bright yellow grains sit, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L13/tuena-gold.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 13 field task · Tuena under the microscope", "q": "What are the bright yellow grains, and where are they sitting?", "seen": "<ul><li>Bright yellow, high-reflectance grains, irregular and elongate.</li><li>Strung along thin fractures, plus a few isolated specks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>gold</b> in micro-veinlets and disseminated grains, filling late fractures: the gold came in after the rock was already deformed and fractured, as the vein history says. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 40</span></p>", "e": "So it’s gold in micro-veinlets and disseminated grains, filling late fractures: the gold came in after the rock was already deformed and fractured, as the vein history says."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-banded", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "what", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/banded.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab of a vein made of many thin, parallel bands of white, grey and pale blue quartz and chalcedony"}], "img": "img/L14/banded.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · a vein specimen", "q": "Describe the banding. What does it tell you about how this vein filled?", "seen": "<ul><li>Many thin, roughly parallel bands of white quartz, grey and translucent blue-grey chalcedony, repeated across the slab.</li><li>No wall-rock fragments, no single coarse fill.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the vein filled bit by bit, band after band, each layer growing on the last in open space: crustiform (colloform) banding. It’s crack-seal behaviour: the vein opens, minerals precipitate, it opens again, and each time the fluid chemistry has changed a little, which is what gives the spectacular banding. Each band records a pulse of fluid, and fine chalcedony means rapid, low-temperature deposition. This is the signature texture of low sulphidation epithermal veins, and gold often sits in particular bands. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 1, 27 to 29</span></p>", "e": "So the vein filled bit by bit, band after band, each layer growing on the last in open space: crustiform (colloform) banding. It’s crack-seal behaviour: the vein opens, minerals precipitate, it opens again, and each time the fluid chemistry has changed a little, which is what gives the spectacular banding. Each band records a pulse of fluid, and fine chalcedony means rapid, low-temperature deposition. This is the signature texture of low sulphidation epithermal veins, and gold often sits in particular bands."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-champagne", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "surface", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/champagne.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A steaming pale green-blue pool with a bright orange rim of precipitate"}], "img": "img/L14/champagne.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · Champagne Pool", "q": "The orange rim of this pool at Waiotapu assays about 80 ppm gold. Why isn’t anyone mining it?", "seen": "<ul><li>A steaming pool with a bright orange rim: precipitated sulphides of arsenic, antimony and mercury, carrying gold and silver.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So 80 ppm (80 g/t) is a high grade, but there’s no <b>volume</b> to exploit: a thin crust on one pool. Look closely and you can see the water boiling: this is a live epithermal system depositing gold right now, the surface end of what, buried and preserved, could become an ore deposit. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 7</span></p>", "e": "So 80 ppm (80 g/t) is a high grade, but there’s no volume to exploit: a thin crust on one pool. Look closely and you can see the water boiling: this is a live epithermal system depositing gold right now, the surface end of what, buried and preserved, could become an ore deposit."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-vughy", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "hs", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/vughy1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale brownish rock full of irregular holes and cavities, a pen for scale", "cap": "A · Veladero, Argentina"}, {"src": "img/L14/vughy2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey rock riddled with small angular holes where crystals used to be, a pen for scale", "cap": "B · El Indio district, Chile"}], "img": "img/L14/vughy1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · a strange porous rock", "q": "The holes in B have the shape of crystals. What was in them, what took it away, and what’s left?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale, light, porous rock; the holes in B are blocky and rectangular, the shape of feldspar phenocrysts in a porphyry.</li><li>A: holes through a fine-grained breccia matrix and fragments.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is <b>vughy silica</b>. Extremely acid fluid (pH below 2) dissolved the feldspar phenocrysts and everything else except silica, leaving a residual silica skeleton with crystal-shaped holes. It’s the core of high sulphidation alteration, and its porosity makes it a great trap for later gold-enargite fluid. A is a diatreme breccia at Veladero, B a vughy silica-altered porphyry in the El Indio district. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 22</span></p>", "e": "So this is vughy silica. Extremely acid fluid (pH below 2) dissolved the feldspar phenocrysts and everything else except silica, leaving a residual silica skeleton with crystal-shaped holes. It’s the core of high sulphidation alteration, and its porosity makes it a great trap for later gold-enargite fluid. A is a diatreme breccia at Veladero, B a vughy silica-altered porphyry in the El Indio district."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-bonanza", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "ls", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/ediecreek.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fingers holding a piece of grey quartz vein with a central band of yellow gold filling the vein centre", "cap": "A · Edie Creek, PNG"}, {"src": "img/L14/porgera.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Grey rock with a mass of fine, wiry, branching gold and pale quartz, a pen for scale", "cap": "B · Porgera Zone VII, PNG"}], "img": "img/L14/ediecreek.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · bonanza", "q": "Where exactly is the gold in A, and what texture does the gold have in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: gold fills the centre line of an open quartz vein, the last space to close.</li><li>B: fine, branching wire gold with quartz and a green vanadium mica, roscoelite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So both are <b>bonanza</b> ore. In A the gold was the last thing deposited, in the open centre of the vein; in B it grew as free wires into open space. Open space plus a sudden change in the fluid (boiling or mixing) gives these spectacular grades. Roscoelite is a good sign of ore in epithermal systems, as fuchsite is in orogenic gold. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 26</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 16 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So both are bonanza ore. In A the gold was the last thing deposited, in the open centre of the vein; in B it grew as free wires into open space. Open space plus a sudden change in the fluid (boiling or mixing) gives these spectacular grades. Roscoelite is a good sign of ore in epithermal systems, as fuchsite is in orogenic gold."},
 {"id": "L14-obs-hishikari", "kind": "obs", "lec": 14, "sec": "ls", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L14/hishikari.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Two geologists in hard hats beside a wall of contorted white and grey banded quartz with darker fragments"}], "img": "img/L14/hishikari.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 14 field task · Hishikari", "q": "Look at the vein margins. Besides the banding, what else is in the vein?", "seen": "<ul><li>Thick, banded, white and grey quartz-adularia, contorted in places.</li><li>Angular dark fragments of wall rock suspended in the vein near its margins.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is a banded adularia-sericite fissure vein with <b>floating clast breccias</b>: wall-rock fragments that broke off and were caught up as the vein grew, a sign of repeated fracturing and fluid surges. It’s really a vein breccia: the vein itself has been brecciated. Hishikari, Japan, first mined for quartz, is one of the world’s richest gold mines and the classic low sulphidation example. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 28 and 29</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 16 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So this is a banded adularia-sericite fissure vein with floating clast breccias: wall-rock fragments that broke off and were caught up as the vein grew, a sign of repeated fracturing and fluid surges. It’s really a vein breccia: the vein itself has been brecciated. Hishikari, Japan, first mined for quartz, is one of the world’s richest gold mines and the classic low sulphidation example."},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-0", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/cass-twin.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Very heavy, hard, adamantine, black, twinned at an angle. Name it.", "o": ["Wolframite", "Cassiterite", "Schorl", "Magnetite"], "a": 1, "e": "Cassiterite, with a classic knee twin. (Elsmore, NSW, slide 6)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-1", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/cass-needle.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Slender prismatic crystals of a very dense tin mineral. Which variety?", "o": ["Wood tin", "Ruby tin", "Needle tin", "Stannite"], "a": 2, "e": "Needle tin: prismatic cassiterite, Bolivia. (slide 6)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-2", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/stannite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Massive, steel-grey metallic tin mineral from a sulphide-rich vein. Name it.", "o": ["Galena", "Stannite", "Molybdenite", "Bismuthinite"], "a": 1, "e": "Stannite, Cu₂FeSnS₄. (Cornwall, slide 10)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-3", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/sch-xl.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Golden orange, vitreous to adamantine, heavy for its colour, pseudo-octahedral. Name it.", "o": ["Fluorite", "Scheelite", "Topaz", "Calcite"], "a": 1, "e": "Scheelite, CaWO₄; check with a UV lamp. (slide 14)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-4", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ferberite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Black, bladed, striated, very heavy, on quartz. Name it.", "o": ["Ferberite (wolframite)", "Schorl", "Cassiterite", "Hematite"], "a": 0, "e": "Ferberite, the Fe end of wolframite. Schorl is lighter, with triangular cross-sections. (slide 16)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-5", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/hubnerite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Red-brown bladed crystals of a heavy tungsten mineral. Name it.", "o": ["Hübnerite", "Ruby tin", "Rutile", "Cinnabar"], "a": 0, "e": "Hübnerite, MnWO₄. (slide 16)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-6", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/bismuthinite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Soft, lead-grey, radiating acicular metallic needles. Name it.", "o": ["Stibnite", "Bismuthinite", "Molybdenite", "Galena"], "a": 1, "e": "Bismuthinite, Bi₂S₃. Stibnite looks very similar; context (granite Sn-W system) helps. (slide 20)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-7", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/bismuth.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Very dense, soft, silvery with a pinkish tint and strong cleavage. Name it.", "o": ["Native bismuth", "Native silver", "Galena", "Arsenopyrite"], "a": 0, "e": "Native bismuth: reddish-white tint, SG ~9.8. (slide 20)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-8", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/molybdenite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Extremely soft, flexible, greasy grey hexagonal plates. Name it.", "o": ["Graphite", "Molybdenite", "Biotite", "Hematite"], "a": 1, "e": "Molybdenite: denser and more metallic than graphite. (Kingsgate, NSW, slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-9", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ferrimolybdite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Bright yellow powder on weathered Mo ore. Name it.", "o": ["Sulphur", "Ferrimolybdite", "Jarosite", "Scheelite"], "a": 1, "e": "Ferrimolybdite, an alteration product of molybdenite. (slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-10", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/ilsemannite.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Pale blue powdery coating on oxidised molybdenite ore. Name it.", "o": ["Azurite", "Chrysocolla", "Ilsemannite", "Fluorite"], "a": 2, "e": "Ilsemannite, a blue molybdenum alteration product. (Carinthia, Austria, slide 23)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-11", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/beryl.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Pale blue hexagonal prism with mica, from a granite system. Name it.", "o": ["Beryl (aquamarine)", "Apatite", "Topaz", "Quartz"], "a": 0, "e": "Beryl, var. aquamarine, with muscovite. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L15-idCards-12", "kind": "mineral", "lec": 15, "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/L15/tourmaline.jpg", "lbl": "Hand specimen", "q": "Black, striated prism with a rounded triangular cross-section. Name it.", "o": ["Ferberite", "Schorl tourmaline", "Hornblende", "Cassiterite"], "a": 1, "e": "Schorl: striations along the length, triangular cross-section, lighter than wolframite. (slide 27)"},
 {"id": "L15-obs-woodtin", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/cass-wood.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A slab with concentric bands of cream and yellow-brown to dark brown material in a wood-grain pattern"}], "img": "img/L15/cass-wood.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · wood tin", "q": "This doesn’t look like any tin ore you’d expect. Describe the banding, and suggest how it formed.", "seen": "<ul><li>Concentric, wavy, wood-grain bands of cream and yellow-brown to dark brown, fibrous in texture.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is <b>wood tin</b>, a fibrous, colloform cassiterite from Cornwall. The bands record sequential deposition from a hydrothermal vein: quartz, then cassiterite, then quartz, then cassiterite, over and over. It looks nothing like cassiterite, which is exactly why it’s worth remembering. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 7</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So this is wood tin, a fibrous, colloform cassiterite from Cornwall. The bands record sequential deposition from a hydrothermal vein: quartz, then cassiterite, then quartz, then cassiterite, over and over. It looks nothing like cassiterite, which is exactly why it’s worth remembering."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-uv", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tungsten", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/sch-massive.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale buff, granular rock with yellow-brown patches", "cap": "A · daylight"}, {"src": "img/L15/sch-uv.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Bright blue-white glowing patches on a black background", "cap": "B · under UV light"}], "img": "img/L15/sch-massive.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · night prospecting", "q": "In daylight A is easy to overlook. What does B show, and why is it useful in the field?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: an unremarkable yellow-brown granular mass.</li><li>B: the same kind of material glowing intense blue-white.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>scheelite</b>, which fluoresces blue-white under short-wave UV. At night, a UV lamp makes scheelite in outcrop, core or a waste dump light up against the minerals around it, a cheap and fast way to find and map tungsten. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 14</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So it’s scheelite, which fluoresces blue-white under short-wave UV. At night, a UV lamp makes scheelite in outcrop, core or a waste dump light up against the minerals around it, a cheap and fast way to find and map tungsten."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-topaz", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "gangue", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/topaz.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A clear, colourless crystal with a rough, pitted and frosted surface instead of sharp faces"}], "img": "img/L15/topaz.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a damaged crystal", "q": "This colourless topaz has lost its sharp faces. What happened to it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Clear, colourless topaz with rounded, frosted, pitted surfaces.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s been <b>etched</b>: partly dissolved by a later, very low-pH hydrothermal fluid. These systems see not one fluid but several pulses, and later ones can attack the minerals that earlier ones grew. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 27</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So it’s been etched: partly dissolved by a later, very low-pH hydrothermal fluid. These systems see not one fluid but several pulses, and later ones can attack the minerals that earlier ones grew."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-greisen", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "greisen", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/greisen.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale cream to yellowish, granular rock with no pink or white feldspar visible"}], "img": "img/L15/greisen.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a greisen", "q": "This started as granite. What minerals are missing, and what replaced them?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale, granular rock, yellowish in places, with no visible pink K-feldspar or white plagioclase.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the feldspars are gone, replaced by <b>quartz and muscovite</b>; the yellowish colour is the muscovite. That’s a greisen, and greisens are where tin and tungsten minerals tend to sit. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 31</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the feldspars are gone, replaced by quartz and muscovite ; the yellowish colour is the muscovite. That’s a greisen, and greisens are where tin and tungsten minerals tend to sit."},
 {"id": "L15-obs-tallebung", "kind": "obs", "lec": 15, "sec": "tallebung", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L15/tb-cass.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Close-up of a white quartz vein with black patches and grains, a scale card beside it"}], "img": "img/L15/tb-cass.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 15 field task · a Tallebung vein", "q": "What are the black patches in this quartz, and why would processing this ore be simple?", "seen": "<ul><li>Milky white quartz with coarse, black, lustrous patches and grains.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the black mineral is <b>cassiterite</b> (with ferberite). Both are coarse and very dense, so you crush the quartz and separate them by gravity, almost like panning. No complicated chemistry needed. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 42</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 26 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the black mineral is cassiterite (with ferberite). Both are coarse and very dense, so you crush the quartz and separate them by gravity, almost like panning. No complicated chemistry needed."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-colloform", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "families", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/colloform.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Cut slab with concentric cream, yellow and brown bands wrapped around dark grey patches, 2 cm scale bar"}], "img": "img/L16/colloform.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · banded zinc ore", "q": "Describe the banding. Did these minerals grow into open space or replace something solid?", "seen": "<ul><li>Concentric, rounded, cream to yellow-brown bands wrapping around darker grey patches, like layers of an onion.</li><li>Branching dark grey growths between some bands.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the pale bands are colloform sphalerite (low-iron, pale) and the branching grey growths are dendritic galena. Both grew into <b>open space</b>, layer by layer. This is MVT ore from Upper Silesia, Poland. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 28</span> (Leach et al., 2005)</p>", "e": "So the pale bands are colloform sphalerite (low-iron, pale) and the branching grey growths are dendritic galena. Both grew into open space, layer by layer. This is MVT ore from Upper Silesia, Poland. (Leach et al., 2005)"},
 {"id": "L16-obs-cmpvcore", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "cmpvmin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/cmpv-core.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Six panels: A, a drill core of pale bleached rock with grey and white veins and dark green patches; B to F, microscope images"}], "img": "img/L16/cmpv-core.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · a CMPV core", "q": "Look at A. Describe the host rock’s colour and what crosses it. What would you log?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale, bleached siltstone (Fe has been removed).</li><li>A quartz vein with siderite and disseminated sulphide, a thin massive sulphide veinlet, and dark green chlorite.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So you’d log a bleached siltstone cut by a quartz-siderite vein with disseminated sulphide and a massive sulphide veinlet, plus chlorite: classic CMPV gangue and ore, with the bleaching as your wider vector. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 13</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 27 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So you’d log a bleached siltstone cut by a quartz-siderite vein with disseminated sulphide and a massive sulphide veinlet, plus chlorite: classic CMPV gangue and ore, with the bleaching as your wider vector."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-webbsrl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "cmpvmin", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/webbs-rl.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light: a broad bright white grain on the right with black triangular pits, irregular pale grey grains on the left with a small yellow patch, all in dark grey gangue, 200 micrometre scale bar"}], "img": "img/L16/webbs-rl.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · Webbs ore in reflected light", "q": "Find the black triangles in the bright grain on the right. Which mineral do they identify, and what is the small yellow patch on the left?", "seen": "<ul><li>A bright white grain with black triangular pits lined up in rows.</li><li>Irregular pale grey grains on the left with a small brassy yellow patch, in dark grey non-reflective gangue.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the bright grain is <b>galena</b>: triangular pits from plucking along its cubic cleavage are its giveaway. The yellow patch is <b>chalcopyrite</b>, and the dark background is quartz-carbonate gangue. Freibergite, arsenopyrite and sphalerite with stannite are the other minerals labelled from Webbs on this slide. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 12</span></p>", "e": "So the bright grain is galena: triangular pits from plucking along its cubic cleavage are its giveaway. The yellow patch is chalcopyrite, and the dark background is quartz-carbonate gangue. Freibergite, arsenopyrite and sphalerite with stannite are the other minerals labelled from Webbs on this slide."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-breccia", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "mvtore", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/mvt-breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A cut slab of angular pale carbonate blocks separated by thin dark sulphide-filled gaps, a coin for scale"}], "img": "img/L16/mvt-breccia.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · a breccia", "q": "The blocks are carbonate, the dark seams sulphide. What made the breccia, and when did the sulphide arrive?", "seen": "<ul><li>Angular blocks of pale carbonate that fit back together, separated by thin dark sulphide seams.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is a <b>dissolution collapse breccia</b>: the carbonate was dissolved, the roof collapsed into blocks, and hydrothermal fluid then altered the breccia and filled and replaced the gaps with sulphide. Collapse first, ore second. Upper Silesia, Poland. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 28</span> (Leach et al., 2005)</p>", "e": "So this is a dissolution collapse breccia: the carbonate was dissolved, the roof collapsed into blocks, and hydrothermal fluid then altered the breccia and filled and replaced the gaps with sulphide. Collapse first, ore second. Upper Silesia, Poland. (Leach et al., 2005)"},
 {"id": "L16-obs-manukapit", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "manuka", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/manuka-pit.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Composite of open-pit photos labelled saprolite, oxide zone, pyritic shale, dolomitic limestone, Gundaroo sandstone and sulfides"}], "img": "img/L16/manuka-pit.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · the pit wall", "q": "From top to bottom, what layers can you see in the pit, and which one carries the silver that’s mined?", "seen": "<ul><li>Weathered saprolite and an oxide zone at the top, then black pyritic shale, then dolomitic limestone, over sandstone.</li><li>Sulphide pockets in the limestone.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the silver being mined is in the <b>oxide zone clays</b> above the limestone. Underneath are the two primary styles: pyrite-rich black shale and galena-sphalerite in the dolomitic limestone. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 49</span> <span class=\"srcs\">lecture recording, 27 Oct</span></p>", "e": "So the silver being mined is in the oxide zone clays above the limestone. Underneath are the two primary styles: pyrite-rich black shale and galena-sphalerite in the dolomitic limestone."},
 {"id": "L16-obs-manukarl", "kind": "obs", "lec": 16, "sec": "manuka", "imgs": [{"src": "img/L16/framboids.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Electron image of clusters of tiny spheres made of even tinier crystals, 10 micrometre scale", "cap": "A · SEM, scale 10 µm"}, {"src": "img/L16/covellite.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Reflected light view with blue patches in a dull olive matrix and a bright yellow speck", "cap": "B · reflected light, scale 50 µm"}], "img": "img/L16/framboids.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Lecture 16 field task · under the microscope", "q": "What are the raspberry-like spheres in A, and the blue mineral in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: spheres a few to tens of µm across, each packed with tiny crystals.</li><li>B: patches with a strong indigo-blue colour in reflected light.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is <b>framboidal pyrite</b>, typical of low-temperature, organic-rich, bacterially influenced sediment such as Manuka’s black shale. B’s blue is <b>covellite</b> (CuS), unmistakable in reflected light; chalcopyrite is rare here. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 51 and 52</span></p>", "e": "So A is framboidal pyrite, typical of low-temperature, organic-rich, bacterially influenced sediment such as Manuka’s black shale. B’s blue is covellite (CuS), unmistakable in reflected light; chalcopyrite is rare here."},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-0", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s036_1.jpg", "lbl": "End of a tray", "q": "What is written here?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 0, "e": "Hole name and tray number, so the tray can always be found and laid out in order. (slide 36)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-1", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s037_1.jpg", "lbl": "Between two pieces of core", "q": "Who put this block here, and why?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 1, "e": "The drillers mark the depth at the end of each run. (slide 37)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-2", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s038_1.jpg", "lbl": "On the side of the tray", "q": "What are the blue ticks?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 2, "e": "Geologist’s metre mark-ups. Check them: mistakes are common. (slides 38 and 51)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-3", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s039_1.jpg", "lbl": "Painted on the core", "q": "What do the white letters and lines show?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 3, "e": "Where a piece was taken for specific gravity (density) testing. (slide 39)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-4", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s040_1.jpg", "lbl": "A red block in the tray", "q": "What does this block record?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 4, "e": "10 cm of hole with no core recovered. (slide 40)"},
 {"id": "log-mkCards-5", "kind": "core", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "lbl": "Drawn along the core", "q": "What is the long line?", "o": ["Tray identification", "Drillers’ depth block", "Metre mark-ups", "SG test mark", "Core loss", "Orientation line"], "a": 5, "e": "The drillers’ orientation line, arrows pointing down hole. (slide 43)"},
 {"id": "log-obs-rabpiles", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "methods", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A drill rig in scrub behind long rows of small piles of crushed rock on red ground, changing colour from red to orange to pale cream along each row"}], "img": "img/logging/s007_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · rows of piles", "q": "What is each little pile, and what does the colour change along a row tell you? How much would you trust these samples?", "seen": "<ul><li>Long rows of small piles of rock chips laid out in front of the rig.</li><li>Along each row the colour changes from red-brown to orange to pale cream.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So each pile is the cuttings from one metre of a RAB hole, and each row is one hole read from the top down: the colour change is you passing down through the weathering profile. Useful for a first look, but RAB samples are low quality. They come up outside the rods, so chips from higher in the hole can mix in. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 7, 9 and 27</span></p>", "e": "So each pile is the cuttings from one metre of a RAB hole, and each row is one hole read from the top down: the colour change is you passing down through the weathering profile. Useful for a first look, but RAB samples are low quality. They come up outside the rods, so chips from higher in the hole can mix in."},
 {"id": "log-obs-oriline", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "orient", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey core in a tray with a long straight red line along it and short angled red strokes off the line"}], "img": "img/logging/s043_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · red lines", "q": "What is the long red line, and what are the short angled strokes for?", "seen": "<ul><li>A straight red line along the length of the core.</li><li>Short red strokes angled off it, like arrowheads, all pointing the same way.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s the orientation line, and the strokes are arrows pointing <b>down the hole</b>. Every structure you measure on this core is measured from that line, so you always know which way was down and which side was the bottom. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 41 to 43</span></p>", "e": "So it’s the orientation line, and the strokes are arrows pointing down the hole. Every structure you measure on this core is measured from that line, so you always know which way was down and which side was the bottom."},
 {"id": "log-obs-weathered", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale orange-pink, finely cracked core beside a centimetre scale", "cap": "A · 12 m"}, {"src": "img/logging/s056_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Broken blocks of yellow-brown and grey rock with a crusty surface beside a scale", "cap": "B · 23.0 m"}], "img": "img/logging/s055_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · the top of the hole", "q": "Both are from the weathered zone. What has happened to A, and what does the slide call the depth in B?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: soft-looking, pale pink to orange, cracked all over, no fresh minerals visible.</li><li>B: broken, crumbly blocks with yellow-brown iron staining and some grey rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is completely weathered rock: <b>kaolinite and hematite</b> from weathering, the clay-and-iron-oxide top of the profile. B marks the <b>base of oxidation</b> at 23 m: below here the sulphides are fresh. Log it carefully, because the oxide and primary ores need different processing. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 55 and 56</span></p>", "e": "So A is completely weathered rock: kaolinite and hematite from weathering, the clay-and-iron-oxide top of the profile. B marks the base of oxidation at 23 m: below here the sulphides are fresh. Log it carefully, because the oxide and primary ores need different processing."},
 {"id": "log-obs-faults", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with a band of crushed, granular grey material running between two blocks, beside a scale card", "cap": "A · 40.4 to 40.6 m"}, {"src": "img/logging/s058_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A tray of several rows of core where one long row is broken into rubble and small fragments", "cap": "B · a whole row of broken core"}], "img": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · two broken zones", "q": "A is a 20 cm crushed zone. B is broken over a long length of core. What are they, and why is B so long?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: a narrow band of crushed, granular material, finer than the rock either side.</li><li>B: rubble and fragments along most of a tray row, with intact core above and below.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is a <b>shear zone with gouge</b>, the ground-up rock along a fault. B is a fault zone running <b>sub-parallel to the core axis</b>: the hole went down along the fault instead of across it, so you get metres of broken core and almost no information about the fault’s orientation. That’s why you angle holes across the expected structures. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 30, 57 and 58</span></p>", "e": "So A is a shear zone with gouge, the ground-up rock along a fault. B is a fault zone running sub-parallel to the core axis: the hole went down along the fault instead of across it, so you get metres of broken core and almost no information about the fault’s orientation. That’s why you angle holes across the expected structures."},
 {"id": "log-obs-folded", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Dark grey core with white veins twisting into tight loops and hooks, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 221 m", "q": "Log the host rock and the veins. What happened to the veins after they formed?", "seen": "<ul><li>Dark grey, fine-grained host rock.</li><li>White veins bent into tight loops and hooks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So these are <b>folded quartz veins in siltstone</b>. The veins formed first, then the rock was deformed and they folded with it, so the veining is older than (or early in) the deformation. That relative timing matters for orogenic gold, where you want to know which vein generation carries the gold. <span class=\"srcs\">slide 59</span></p>", "e": "So these are folded quartz veins in siltstone. The veins formed first, then the rock was deformed and they folded with it, so the veining is older than (or early in) the deformation. That relative timing matters for orogenic gold, where you want to know which vein generation carries the gold."},
 {"id": "log-obs-gypsum", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pinkish-grey core crossed by a thin pale curved vein, with a red line along the top of the core, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 802.6 m", "q": "The thin pale vein is gypsum. Estimate its alpha angle. Is it a good structure to measure?", "seen": "<ul><li>A thin pale vein crossing the core almost straight across.</li><li>A red line along the core: the orientation (or cut) line.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So the vein is at about <b>80° to the core axis</b>, nearly perpendicular. It cuts right through the core, so it’s measurable: alpha 80°, and with the orientation line you could get beta too. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 47 and 60</span></p>", "e": "So the vein is at about 80° to the core axis, nearly perpendicular. It cuts right through the core, so it’s measurable: alpha 80°, and with the orientation line you could get beta too."},
 {"id": "log-obs-vg", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "intervals", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s061_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fine-grained grey metallic core with several small blue pen circles drawn on it"}], "img": "img/logging/s061_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · 375.9 m", "q": "The core is massive sulphide. What do you think the blue circles mark, and why bother drawing them?", "seen": "<ul><li>Fine-grained, grey, metallic rock with no gangue to speak of.</li><li>Small blue circles drawn around tiny bright specks.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>massive sulphide with visible gold</b>, each grain circled in blue. Visible gold is logged and marked so nobody misses it and the interval is sampled carefully. Because of the nugget effect, gold core is often assayed whole rather than halved. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 17 and 61</span></p>", "e": "So it’s massive sulphide with visible gold, each grain circled in blue. Visible gold is logged and marked so nobody misses it and the interval is sampled carefully. Because of the nugget effect, gold core is often assayed whole rather than halved."},
 {"id": "log-obs-bowdens", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "storage", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core broken into chunks with brown and orange staining and dark patches", "cap": "A"}, {"src": "img/logging/s063_2.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Fresh greenish-grey core with pale angular fragments in a fine matrix", "cap": "B"}], "img": "img/logging/s063_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · same deposit, different years", "q": "Both are tuff-breccia from the Bowdens silver deposit. One was drilled in 2021, the other in 2025. Which is which, and what happened to the older one?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: brown and orange stained, broken, the original texture hard to see.</li><li>B: fresh, greenish grey, with clear pale fragments in a fine matrix.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A is the older core, drilled in <b>2021</b> (164.7 m), and B the <b>2025</b> core (102.5 m). A’s sulphides have oxidised in storage, staining it and breaking it up; a few years in a core yard was enough. Log and photograph core as soon as it comes out. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 62 and 63</span></p>", "e": "So A is the older core, drilled in 2021 (164.7 m), and B the 2025 core (102.5 m). A’s sulphides have oxidised in storage, staining it and breaking it up; a few years in a core yard was enough. Log and photograph core as soon as it comes out."},
 {"id": "log-obs-decay", "kind": "obs", "page": "logging.html", "pageName": "Drill core logging", "sec": "storage", "imgs": [{"src": "img/logging/s065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A tray of pale cream core crazed with cracks like dried mud", "cap": "A · slide 65"}, {"src": "img/logging/s067_1.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Core with patches of bright sky-blue crust on pale and red-brown rock, beside a scale", "cap": "B · slide 67"}], "img": "img/logging/s065_1.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Drill core logging field task · two more casualties", "q": "What has gone wrong in each tray?", "seen": "<ul><li>A: pale core crazed with cracks all over, falling apart.</li><li>B: bright blue crusts growing on the core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So A has been destroyed by abundant fine-grained, disseminated <b>pyrite and marcasite</b> oxidising (marcasite is especially unstable). B is encrusted with <b>copper sulphates</b> that grew after drilling as copper sulphides oxidised. Neither tells you anything about the original ore unless someone logged it first. <span class=\"srcs\">slides 65 to 67</span></p>", "e": "So A has been destroyed by abundant fine-grained, disseminated pyrite and marcasite oxidising (marcasite is especially unstable). B is encrusted with copper sulphates that grew after drilling as copper sulphides oxidised. Neither tells you anything about the original ore unless someone logged it first."},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-0", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s057_1.jpg", "lbl": "Type", "q": "What type of structure is the crushed band?", "o": ["Bedding", "Shear zone with gouge", "Cross-bedding"], "a": 1, "e": "Ground-up rock along a fault or shear. (logging deck, slide 57)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-1", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s059_1.jpg", "lbl": "Primary or secondary?", "q": "Are the folded veins primary or secondary structures?", "o": ["Primary", "Secondary"], "a": 1, "e": "Veins and folds form after the rock does, so both are secondary. (logging deck, slide 59)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-2", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s060_1.jpg", "lbl": "Spacing", "q": "How would you describe the spacing of veining in this piece?", "o": ["Closely spaced fracture zone", "A single, isolated vein", "Pervasive"], "a": 1, "e": "One thin gypsum vein: note its angle (about 80° to the core axis). (logging deck, slide 60)"},
 {"id": "alt-stCards-3", "kind": "core", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "eoh", "img": "img/logging/s058_1.jpg", "lbl": "Mining impact", "q": "A broad fault zone of broken core like this matters to the mine because...", "o": ["It is a zone of weakness that can cause ground failure", "It makes the ore richer", "It has no effect"], "a": 0, "e": "Faults and fracture zones are engineering hazards. (lab notes)"},
 {"id": "alt-obs-dominion", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "oxid", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/gossan.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A geologist in a hard hat crouching in a shallow pit dug into red-brown soil and rubble in scrubland, with sample bags beside the pit"}], "img": "img/alteration/gossan.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · a gold gossan", "q": "This pit is dug into an exposed gossan in the southern Cobar Basin. What colour is the material, and which iron oxide would you expect to dominate it?", "seen": "<ul><li>Red-brown to orange-brown soil and rubble, much redder than the scrubland around it.</li><li>Sample bags lined up beside the pit.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So this is the Dominion Prospect gold gossan, and the red-brown colour points to <b>hematite</b> and goethite from oxidised sulphides. Reddish hematite suggests moving water. The bags are there because gossans are sampled for the metals that stay behind, gold among them. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, oxidation</span></p>", "e": "So this is the Dominion Prospect gold gossan, and the red-brown colour points to hematite and goethite from oxidised sulphides. Reddish hematite suggests moving water. The bags are there because gossans are sampled for the metals that stay behind, gold among them."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-busai", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A grey-green outcrop surface with yellow-green patches, a geological hammer lying across it"}], "img": "img/alteration/propylitic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Busai, Woodlark Island", "q": "Name the two colours in this rock and the minerals that make them.", "seen": "<ul><li>Patchy yellow-green and duller grey to olive green over the whole surface.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>propylitic</b> alteration: yellow-green <b>epidote</b> and grey-green <b>chlorite</b>. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, propylitic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s propylitic alteration: yellow-green epidote and grey-green chlorite."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-hera", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/phyllic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Underground face with a pale grey to white layer above dark rock, a scale card at the bottom"}], "img": "img/alteration/phyllic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Hera mine", "q": "The pale layer was once a dark siltstone. What gives it its colour and sheen?", "seen": "<ul><li>A pale, almost white band with a soft sheen, sharply above darker rock.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>phyllic</b>-altered siltstone, made of fine illite-muscovite and quartz. Phyllosilicates crystallising in the rock give it that sheen. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, phyllic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s phyllic -altered siltstone, made of fine illite-muscovite and quartz. Phyllosilicates crystallising in the rock give it that sheen."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-drake", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A section of drill core with a pale pink to salmon colour and fine grey speckles, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/alteration/potassic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Drake goldfield", "q": "What mineral makes this core pink?", "seen": "<ul><li>Pale pink to salmon core with fine grey patches.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>potassic</b> alteration: the pink comes from hydrothermal <b>K-feldspar</b>, commonly the low-temperature variety adularia. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, potassic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s potassic alteration: the pink comes from hydrothermal K-feldspar, commonly the low-temperature variety adularia."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-bowdensarg", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/argillic.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "Pale grey, crumbly, soft-looking core with a rough surface, beside a scale"}], "img": "img/alteration/argillic.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Bowdens silver deposit", "q": "The core is pale and crumbly. What happened to its feldspar?", "seen": "<ul><li>Very pale grey, rough and crumbly, weak-looking core.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>argillic</b> alteration with abundant <b>illite</b>: the feldspar has broken down to clay, which bleaches and badly weakens the rock. <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, argillic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s argillic alteration with abundant illite: the feldspar has broken down to clay, which bleaches and badly weakens the rock."},
 {"id": "alt-obs-bukit", "kind": "obs", "page": "alteration.html", "pageName": "Alteration lab", "sec": "types", "imgs": [{"src": "img/alteration/advarg.jpg", "mic": false, "alt": "A cut slab with pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown patches, beside a ruler"}], "img": "img/alteration/advarg.jpg", "mic": false, "lbl": "Alteration lab field task · Bukit Jambi, Sumatra", "q": "Four colours: pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown. Match each to a mineral.", "seen": "<ul><li>Pure white, yellow-white, red and yellow-brown patches swirled together.</li></ul> <p class=\"so\">So it’s <b>advanced argillic</b> alteration: <b>alunite</b> (yellow-white), <b>hematite</b> (red), <b>goethite</b> (yellow-brown) and <b>kaolinite</b> (pure white). <span class=\"srcs\">lab notes, advanced argillic</span></p>", "e": "So it’s advanced argillic alteration: alunite (yellow-white), hematite (red), goethite (yellow-brown) and kaolinite (pure white)."}
];
